WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.456
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:17.250
MAGNANTI: Welcome, everyone.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:20.612
Delighted to have you with us,
those here at MIT and those

00:00:20.612 --> 00:00:21.195
at a distance.

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:25.020
My name is Tom Magnanti.

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:27.300
I'm one of the co-directors
of the System Design

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:28.980
and Management program.

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:30.870
And it's my pleasure
to welcome you

00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:33.840
to our first
distinguished lecture

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:37.080
series in complex systems.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:39.780
We are very fortunate
to have with us

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:42.150
today and over this
semester Professor Thomas

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:46.500
Hughes, distinguished
professor, and I

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:48.150
think it's probably
appropriate to say,

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:52.110
dean of the history of
technology in the United States

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:53.880
and maybe in the world.

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:58.770
Professor Hughes is well
known for his insightful look

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:03.570
at technology, particularly
look at large scale projects

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:08.190
and of developing a framework
for looking at these projects

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:10.620
and for providing
insight for all of us

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:14.370
at technical institutions
such as MIT and elsewhere.

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:17.142
We're just delighted to have
Professor Hughes with us

00:01:17.142 --> 00:01:17.850
on this occasion.

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:20.570
He's going to give
two public addresses.

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:23.280
The first address will be
today, and the second address

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:24.737
will be on October 30.

00:01:24.737 --> 00:01:26.820
And he will also be meeting
with the System Design

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:30.570
and Management students in
a more intimate setting.

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:34.750
I'd just like to,
for those who are not

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:37.280
familiar with the System
Design and Management program,

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:41.440
it's an educational
program at MIT,

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:45.790
whose intent is to educate
future technical leaders

00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:49.030
in architecting, engineering,
and designing complex systems

00:01:49.030 --> 00:01:50.440
and complex products.

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:53.530
The intellectual
content of this program

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:56.110
revolves around complexity,
complex systems,

00:01:56.110 --> 00:01:57.730
and complex products.

00:01:57.730 --> 00:02:00.210
There clearly are many
ways to look at complexity.

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:02.660
There's various modeling
approaches to looking at it.

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:05.210
There's engineering
ways of looking at it.

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:09.100
But certainly among those viable
ways of looking at technology

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:12.580
is a historical perspective of
trying to understand lessons

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:15.970
from history, what we've
learned from the evolution

00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:19.780
of our understanding of complex
systems, our understanding

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:21.100
of technology.

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:23.949
And Professor Hughes, I
think, brings a rather unique

00:02:23.949 --> 00:02:25.990
perspective on that in
terms of his understanding

00:02:25.990 --> 00:02:28.270
of particular large
scale projects.

00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:31.240
He has written many
books and many articles.

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:35.610
His most recent book
is Rescuing Prometheus.

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:37.750
It's a very notable book.

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:40.680
AUDIENCE: I hate to interrupt,
but the audio is very poor.

00:02:43.602 --> 00:02:45.060
MAGNANTI: We hope
we'll improve it.

00:02:48.908 --> 00:02:51.790
I'd like for the control room
to see if we can improve it.

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:53.950
His most recent book,
Rescuing Prometheus,

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:55.630
has gotten a great
deal of attention.

00:02:55.630 --> 00:02:58.060
It was, I think, a
month, a month and a half

00:02:58.060 --> 00:03:01.600
ago was reviewed in
the New York Times.

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:03.370
Two weeks ago, he was
on National Public

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:06.760
Radio to talk about this
book and got quite a rousing

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:09.190
responses, I understand from
my conversation with him

00:03:09.190 --> 00:03:10.940
a few minutes ago.

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:15.220
He is also very much
intertwined with the system--

00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:18.670
AUDIENCE: We can barely hear.

00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:20.785
MAGNANTI: Let me try
another microphone then.

00:03:20.785 --> 00:03:21.990
HUGHES: You want this?

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:30.870
MAGNANTI: I hope this
is a little bit better.

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:33.630
He's intimately involved,
has been intimately involved

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:36.600
with the System Design
and Management program.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:39.750
I think about six
years ago or so now,

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:43.290
we had a committee at
MIT that was convened

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:46.830
by our previous dean of
engineering provost Joel Moses

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:50.100
and Dan Roos, our associate dean
for engineering systems, that

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:53.040
was looking at
large scale systems.

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:56.340
And we brought together,
at that time, engineers,

00:03:56.340 --> 00:04:00.090
management people from MIT,
anthropologists, historians

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:02.760
to try to get our arms around
this whole issue of large scale

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:03.720
systems.

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:06.480
And I think that was a previous
occasion in which Professor

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:07.710
Hughes was visiting MIT.

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:09.930
And he joined us in
those deliberations.

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:11.610
So I think it's fair
to say that he's

00:04:11.610 --> 00:04:15.240
had a touch, from the beginning,
on the overall direction

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:18.616
and orientation of this program
System Design and Management.

00:04:18.616 --> 00:04:20.490
I think those of us
involved with the program

00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:22.590
couldn't think of anyone
better to inaugurate

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:26.670
this distinguished lecture
series on complex systems

00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:30.056
than Professor Hughes, who will
tell us a little bit about, I

00:04:30.056 --> 00:04:31.680
think, some of the
elements of his book

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:33.888
and some of the projects
that he's been involved with

00:04:33.888 --> 00:04:36.810
and provide us with his
own unique perspective

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:37.500
on these issues.

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:38.208
Professor Hughes.

00:04:51.242 --> 00:04:52.200
HUGHES: Thank you, Tom.

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:53.160
This all right?

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:55.760
Coming through all right?

00:04:55.760 --> 00:04:58.000
Very generous introduction.

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:01.230
I'm very happy to
be here talking

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:05.040
to the people about whom I
write, that is engineers.

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:08.700
Historians do not have this
opportunity very often.

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:11.640
I'm very fortunate to
have it this afternoon

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:14.130
and on other occasions.

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:17.460
Tom mentioned this
National Public Radio.

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:20.080
He said I got a good response.

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:21.660
I'm not sure it was
a good response.

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:23.577
I got an interesting response.

00:05:23.577 --> 00:05:25.410
I'll tell you about it
very briefly before I

00:05:25.410 --> 00:05:29.920
go into my prepared remarks.

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:33.990
The program is called
the Public Issue,

00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:36.120
Public Concerns, Public Issue.

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:38.580
It comes on every
day in the week.

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:42.120
And after the guest
described is interviewed

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:46.500
about a book or
some other activity,

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:49.680
then the audience out
there in radio land

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:54.130
has an opportunity to
call in and ask questions.

00:05:54.130 --> 00:05:58.400
And I was getting a
rather expected, what

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:02.180
I expected by way of
questions, until somebody

00:06:02.180 --> 00:06:07.310
from Pennsylvania named Mike, I
believe, called in and he said,

00:06:07.310 --> 00:06:12.830
professor, I don't want you
to think that I'm a crock.

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:17.630
But he said, when I was
young, aliens abducted me.

00:06:17.630 --> 00:06:20.150
This is on National
Public Radio.

00:06:20.150 --> 00:06:22.190
Aliens abducted me.

00:06:22.190 --> 00:06:24.520
I spent time with them.

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:26.690
And I know in your
book, you write

00:06:26.690 --> 00:06:31.730
about intercontinental ballistic
missiles and computer networks.

00:06:31.730 --> 00:06:33.860
And I happen to know from
being with the aliens

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:37.490
that all of this is
reverse alien engineering.

00:06:40.370 --> 00:06:46.490
Well, he said, did you find
any evidence to support this?

00:06:46.490 --> 00:06:48.680
So the person who
was interviewing me

00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:50.870
was rolling her eyes.

00:06:50.870 --> 00:06:53.570
And all I could
think of saying was,

00:06:53.570 --> 00:06:58.120
I've only been able to
interview earthlings.

00:06:58.120 --> 00:07:01.030
And I said, but you
have these contacts.

00:07:01.030 --> 00:07:06.980
And if you can renew them and
do some serious interviewing,

00:07:06.980 --> 00:07:09.440
then turn these
over to historians,

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:13.910
we can use this to revise
our interpretation.

00:07:13.910 --> 00:07:15.697
So this is true.

00:07:15.697 --> 00:07:17.780
I mean, if he comes through
with those interviews,

00:07:17.780 --> 00:07:18.740
we will revise our.

00:07:18.740 --> 00:07:20.120
Interpretations.

00:07:20.120 --> 00:07:23.390
So he seemed quite satisfied.

00:07:23.390 --> 00:07:29.670
Today, my subject is systems,
does creating systems,

00:07:29.670 --> 00:07:34.490
creating large complex
systems, as Tom said.

00:07:34.490 --> 00:07:38.350
First, a word about
the historical approach

00:07:38.350 --> 00:07:41.750
of which you will
share with me today.

00:07:41.750 --> 00:07:45.440
I think what the historian
can do can help engineers

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:47.280
in several ways.

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:52.070
One way is to put an
engineer's present work

00:07:52.070 --> 00:07:55.220
into some perspective.

00:07:55.220 --> 00:08:00.740
I hope today, when I describe
the creation of several very

00:08:00.740 --> 00:08:04.670
large systems, that you
will be, those of you who

00:08:04.670 --> 00:08:07.830
are into system
design and management,

00:08:07.830 --> 00:08:11.720
will be drawing
analogies on your own

00:08:11.720 --> 00:08:16.760
with what I'm describing
historical cases with what

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:20.450
you experience in your
work today and yesterday

00:08:20.450 --> 00:08:21.920
and tomorrow.

00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:25.670
And incidentally, if you
find that my description

00:08:25.670 --> 00:08:29.960
of these two systems, the Edison
electric light and power system

00:08:29.960 --> 00:08:34.100
and the ARPANET system, their
creation, I should stress,

00:08:34.100 --> 00:08:37.010
don't hesitate to
interrupt me and point out

00:08:37.010 --> 00:08:39.110
where you think I
might have gone astray

00:08:39.110 --> 00:08:44.920
in my interpretations of the
creation of these systems.

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:47.860
I'll be giving, as Tom
said, several lectures,

00:08:47.860 --> 00:08:52.900
so I have a few
opening definitions.

00:08:52.900 --> 00:08:55.960
These definitions I
wish to use initially

00:08:55.960 --> 00:09:00.670
are definitions of closed
systems and open systems.

00:09:00.670 --> 00:09:06.300
Now I've read the
biographical sketches

00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:08.760
of a number of those
of you who are now here

00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:13.590
in the open systems program
or in the Systems Design

00:09:13.590 --> 00:09:15.270
Management program.

00:09:15.270 --> 00:09:18.310
And I know some of you
are in product design,

00:09:18.310 --> 00:09:21.540
and others of you are
in what I am talking

00:09:21.540 --> 00:09:24.600
about today, managing projects.

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:30.240
But I assume that those
of you in product design,

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:35.640
often the activity you are
engaged in is called a project.

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:40.320
And so I believe what I will
say today, even though it's not

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:42.840
about products, say, in
the automobile industry

00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:46.470
or products in the photographic
industry, it is about products.

00:09:46.470 --> 00:09:48.840
Now here is a product.

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:54.650
I call this a closed system.

00:09:54.650 --> 00:09:58.190
It could be called
a technical system.

00:09:58.190 --> 00:10:01.850
In a moment, when I give you
a visual of an open system,

00:10:01.850 --> 00:10:03.620
you will see the distinction.

00:10:03.620 --> 00:10:10.460
Now this diagram is from a
technical journal around 1892,

00:10:10.460 --> 00:10:14.480
I believe, something
in that period.

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:17.120
It's an electric light and
power systems, obviously.

00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:22.520
What I think you might note
here is that, as early as 1982,

00:10:22.520 --> 00:10:26.480
engineers and
managers were quite

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:32.030
capable of combining
heterogeneous components

00:10:32.030 --> 00:10:35.270
into a general system.

00:10:35.270 --> 00:10:37.610
You will notice that
some of the appliances

00:10:37.610 --> 00:10:42.200
here are run on
very high voltage,

00:10:42.200 --> 00:10:44.390
like the industrial motors.

00:10:44.390 --> 00:10:49.070
Some on low voltage like
the electric lights.

00:10:49.070 --> 00:10:51.080
Some have one frequency.

00:10:51.080 --> 00:10:54.740
Other appliances or components
have another frequency.

00:10:54.740 --> 00:10:59.060
But by 1895 or
thereabouts, engineers

00:10:59.060 --> 00:11:02.720
had developed what I think you
might call gateway devices,

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:05.900
transformers, motor
generator sets,

00:11:05.900 --> 00:11:08.540
and so forth that allowed
heterogeneous components

00:11:08.540 --> 00:11:10.730
to be put on a single system.

00:11:10.730 --> 00:11:15.170
So it is a closed system in
that it's a technical system.

00:11:15.170 --> 00:11:17.550
But it is complex.

00:11:17.550 --> 00:11:19.995
And now I would like
to show an open system.

00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:26.090
Now what I hope I can
contribute to your program

00:11:26.090 --> 00:11:33.080
and through other talks
that I shall give at MIT

00:11:33.080 --> 00:11:37.550
is opening your minds, if
they're not already open,

00:11:37.550 --> 00:11:42.390
to the importance
of open systems.

00:11:42.390 --> 00:11:45.750
Open systems, as you
see from the diagram,

00:11:45.750 --> 00:11:49.290
include organizational
components along

00:11:49.290 --> 00:11:52.360
with technical components.

00:11:52.360 --> 00:11:54.610
And a number of system
builders, engineers,

00:11:54.610 --> 00:11:58.070
and managers over history,
over a long period

00:11:58.070 --> 00:12:02.270
of time in Germany and
in the United States

00:12:02.270 --> 00:12:05.180
and elsewhere in the
world, some of these people

00:12:05.180 --> 00:12:09.860
have been quite good in
designing open systems

00:12:09.860 --> 00:12:12.200
and creating open systems.

00:12:12.200 --> 00:12:16.520
Now my impression is that
in most engineering schools,

00:12:16.520 --> 00:12:19.920
I'm not sure about
MIT, I'm fairly sure

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:23.560
though, the emphasis is
not put upon open systems.

00:12:23.560 --> 00:12:26.080
They are considered soft.

00:12:26.080 --> 00:12:29.920
The emphasis is on the
closed technical systems.

00:12:29.920 --> 00:12:32.950
But from my interviewing
of a number of engineers

00:12:32.950 --> 00:12:37.240
in the large complex
systems world,

00:12:37.240 --> 00:12:41.020
I find that a number of you,
perhaps certainly they're

00:12:41.020 --> 00:12:44.290
compatriots, your
compatriots, your peers, whom

00:12:44.290 --> 00:12:48.410
I have interviewed,
are in fact designing,

00:12:48.410 --> 00:12:50.279
developing open systems.

00:12:50.279 --> 00:12:51.820
And as a matter of
fact, they tell me

00:12:51.820 --> 00:12:53.530
they enjoy it very much.

00:12:53.530 --> 00:12:55.180
They are messy.

00:12:55.180 --> 00:12:57.250
And some people consider
their work soft.

00:12:57.250 --> 00:13:03.010
It's not eloquent and elegant
and as quantitative as closed

00:13:03.010 --> 00:13:03.680
systems.

00:13:03.680 --> 00:13:07.810
But it is designing these
is a challenge, which

00:13:07.810 --> 00:13:09.910
young engineers enjoy.

00:13:09.910 --> 00:13:12.620
I'm thinking especially
of engineers I enjoyed--

00:13:12.620 --> 00:13:14.480
I'm sorry, yes, I enjoyed.

00:13:14.480 --> 00:13:18.149
I interviewed on the Central
Artery and Tunnel Project.

00:13:18.149 --> 00:13:19.690
And I would ask
them, how do you feel

00:13:19.690 --> 00:13:24.820
about this bringing into
the design of the system,

00:13:24.820 --> 00:13:28.450
environmental controls, taking
into account the neighborhood

00:13:28.450 --> 00:13:33.610
politics, actually rerouting
the system because of community

00:13:33.610 --> 00:13:34.800
concerns?

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:38.410
And generally, they
said it makes the design

00:13:38.410 --> 00:13:40.780
project much more interesting.

00:13:40.780 --> 00:13:43.150
But they added, several of
the people I interviewed,

00:13:43.150 --> 00:13:48.580
that some of the older persons
did not find this congenial

00:13:48.580 --> 00:13:51.880
and would not
continue the project.

00:13:51.880 --> 00:13:54.470
Now let's take a look at
this for just a moment.

00:13:54.470 --> 00:13:57.040
What I wish I had up
here in this diagram

00:13:57.040 --> 00:14:03.410
was a consulting engineering
firm and also a utility.

00:14:03.410 --> 00:14:07.310
A utility organization
should certainly be there.

00:14:07.310 --> 00:14:12.620
And if I'd had those two and
I'd put those two into my chart,

00:14:12.620 --> 00:14:16.880
then I could say this is
very much like the system

00:14:16.880 --> 00:14:19.250
that the head of German
General Electric,

00:14:19.250 --> 00:14:23.090
which was called Allgemeine
Elektricitats-Gesellschaft.

00:14:23.090 --> 00:14:26.930
The open system that Allgemeine
Elektricitats-Gesellschaft,

00:14:26.930 --> 00:14:34.250
AEG, created in Germany
around 1910, it was a system

00:14:34.250 --> 00:14:37.670
presided over by a man
named Walter Rathenau, who

00:14:37.670 --> 00:14:41.540
was an open system builder
and very good at it.

00:14:41.540 --> 00:14:44.300
Now let me just spend
a moment on this.

00:14:44.300 --> 00:14:47.580
The point here is
let's look at the bank.

00:14:47.580 --> 00:14:51.560
A man like Rathenau, when
he created the system

00:14:51.560 --> 00:14:54.370
and then later used
it, and he was trained

00:14:54.370 --> 00:14:56.330
as a physicist and an engineer.

00:14:56.330 --> 00:14:57.950
He was the head
of German General

00:14:57.950 --> 00:15:04.060
Electric, which was not related
to American General Electric.

00:15:04.060 --> 00:15:09.780
Rathenau wanted some
control over the bank.

00:15:09.780 --> 00:15:12.570
He also wanted some control
of the consulting engineering

00:15:12.570 --> 00:15:14.150
company.

00:15:14.150 --> 00:15:16.650
And let's take the
bank, for example.

00:15:16.650 --> 00:15:21.110
If his consulting engineering
and construction company, which

00:15:21.110 --> 00:15:26.510
he owned stock in, or his
company owned stock in,

00:15:26.510 --> 00:15:30.020
he have them stock in, if
construction activity had

00:15:30.020 --> 00:15:35.120
fallen off, then if he had
influence over the bank,

00:15:35.120 --> 00:15:40.230
he would urge the bank to
lower its interest rates.

00:15:40.230 --> 00:15:44.900
And so that those considering
construction projects

00:15:44.900 --> 00:15:49.150
would be more enticed
by the money market.

00:15:49.150 --> 00:15:51.940
And so there was interaction,
and Rathenau saw it,

00:15:51.940 --> 00:15:56.770
between a bank and a
construction firm in the field.

00:15:56.770 --> 00:15:58.720
And also in this
diagram there should

00:15:58.720 --> 00:16:02.270
be a manufacturing company.

00:16:02.270 --> 00:16:05.830
And Rathenau, through
sitting on a number of boards

00:16:05.830 --> 00:16:09.260
of directors, had influence
in manufacturing companies

00:16:09.260 --> 00:16:10.530
and in utilities.

00:16:10.530 --> 00:16:15.610
And he brought about a symbiosis
between what the utilities were

00:16:15.610 --> 00:16:17.300
needing by way of
equipment and what

00:16:17.300 --> 00:16:20.770
the electrical
manufacturers were producing

00:16:20.770 --> 00:16:21.850
or would produce.

00:16:21.850 --> 00:16:25.810
So what we have here are
functional interrelationships.

00:16:25.810 --> 00:16:29.040
And some persons
have the vision,

00:16:29.040 --> 00:16:31.060
as we will see in a
moment in our world

00:16:31.060 --> 00:16:36.010
today, to put together
these various components

00:16:36.010 --> 00:16:38.090
into an interacting system.

00:16:38.090 --> 00:16:40.510
One thing driving these
people who do this,

00:16:40.510 --> 00:16:42.790
and to me they're
fascinating people,

00:16:42.790 --> 00:16:45.610
and I think, with
all due respect,

00:16:45.610 --> 00:16:48.550
that Mr. Gates is
one of these people,

00:16:48.550 --> 00:16:51.250
these people want control.

00:16:51.250 --> 00:16:54.100
And they want control not
for nefarious reasons,

00:16:54.100 --> 00:16:56.770
but they want control in
order to get the system

00:16:56.770 --> 00:17:01.150
to function in an optimal way
so that they drive control.

00:17:01.150 --> 00:17:05.849
Now if I can have
the next visual.

00:17:05.849 --> 00:17:13.530
This is from The New Yorker
magazine several months ago.

00:17:13.530 --> 00:17:16.790
And here, we have
media companies.

00:17:16.790 --> 00:17:22.790
And you know one of the
media companies is Microsoft.

00:17:22.790 --> 00:17:25.010
And I think most of you
are well aware of this,

00:17:25.010 --> 00:17:27.770
but maybe I should
remind you of it,

00:17:27.770 --> 00:17:33.650
that Microsoft is creating these
functional interrelationships.

00:17:33.650 --> 00:17:38.840
And perhaps one of the driving
instincts of Bill Gates

00:17:38.840 --> 00:17:44.750
is to create these
kinds of interactions

00:17:44.750 --> 00:17:47.750
to have this type of control.

00:17:47.750 --> 00:17:51.800
If you run down Microsoft,
just Microsoft either

00:17:51.800 --> 00:17:54.920
through direct ownership,
through stock ownership,

00:17:54.920 --> 00:17:58.140
or some other mode of
influence, as you will see,

00:17:58.140 --> 00:18:00.320
is into cables, TV
and film production,

00:18:00.320 --> 00:18:05.280
internet technology, internet
content, and so forth.

00:18:05.280 --> 00:18:11.840
And you see these are all media
engagements, media activities.

00:18:11.840 --> 00:18:14.990
And you can understand that
he has a mode of distribution,

00:18:14.990 --> 00:18:17.450
say, the internet,
it's an instinct

00:18:17.450 --> 00:18:20.510
to get control of
the content that

00:18:20.510 --> 00:18:24.900
goes over the distribution,
over the media,

00:18:24.900 --> 00:18:27.120
over the modes of distribution.

00:18:27.120 --> 00:18:30.830
But if we went back to the
other diagram, German General

00:18:30.830 --> 00:18:35.510
Electric in 1910, you
had the same phenomenon.

00:18:35.510 --> 00:18:39.200
You had the
distribution system that

00:18:39.200 --> 00:18:41.480
was also the
generation of content,

00:18:41.480 --> 00:18:45.880
that is electrons, and then et
cetera, et cetera, et cetera.

00:18:45.880 --> 00:18:47.750
And so these are open systems.

00:18:47.750 --> 00:18:51.530
I have another very
complex diagram here.

00:18:51.530 --> 00:18:53.030
We can show the next one Leon.

00:18:58.500 --> 00:19:01.350
This also is from
The New Yorker.

00:19:01.350 --> 00:19:05.550
And the question being asked
by the person writing the essay

00:19:05.550 --> 00:19:09.360
and doing the diagram
is, are these media

00:19:09.360 --> 00:19:15.140
concerns, which I showed you a
moment ago, are they competing?

00:19:15.140 --> 00:19:18.650
Are they collaborating?

00:19:18.650 --> 00:19:24.320
And this diagram shows
companies that are owned

00:19:24.320 --> 00:19:28.790
or companies in which
control is influenced

00:19:28.790 --> 00:19:37.880
that connect the spiders, the
bugs across the lines drawn

00:19:37.880 --> 00:19:39.170
between them.

00:19:39.170 --> 00:19:40.490
And you could study that.

00:19:40.490 --> 00:19:43.250
And you'll see a number of
companies jointly owned,

00:19:43.250 --> 00:19:45.240
et cetera, et cetera, et cetera.

00:19:45.240 --> 00:19:47.760
So this is a very
complex open system.

00:19:47.760 --> 00:19:50.150
And again, the reason
it's an open system

00:19:50.150 --> 00:19:53.510
is because organizations
are part of the system,

00:19:53.510 --> 00:19:56.090
I repeat, and there are
technical components

00:19:56.090 --> 00:19:59.030
in the system.

00:19:59.030 --> 00:20:03.040
Now this is what's called
a web of collaboration.

00:20:05.880 --> 00:20:08.295
Now a word about projects.

00:20:13.350 --> 00:20:16.110
I would argue, and
perhaps some of my friends

00:20:16.110 --> 00:20:20.310
here from the Sloan School
would not accept my argument,

00:20:20.310 --> 00:20:25.450
have some in the audience who
would not accept the argument,

00:20:25.450 --> 00:20:29.320
but it seems to me from reading
the history of engineering

00:20:29.320 --> 00:20:34.050
management, my concern
is with the management

00:20:34.050 --> 00:20:37.080
that has a engineering core.

00:20:37.080 --> 00:20:40.770
And often the people I interview
and the companies I write about

00:20:40.770 --> 00:20:44.460
are managed by persons with
engineering backgrounds.

00:20:44.460 --> 00:20:46.800
So that's the type of
management I'm speaking of now.

00:20:49.800 --> 00:20:53.480
It seems to me looking
back over 100 years

00:20:53.480 --> 00:20:58.160
that projects are
becoming increasingly

00:20:58.160 --> 00:21:03.410
the activity in which
engineers are involved.

00:21:03.410 --> 00:21:07.520
Projects, and as I said,
projects can certainly

00:21:07.520 --> 00:21:10.490
include product development.

00:21:10.490 --> 00:21:14.540
Let me spend a few
more moments on this.

00:21:14.540 --> 00:21:20.830
If one were an engineer, imagine
yourself an engineer in 1920,

00:21:20.830 --> 00:21:23.000
and you'd accepted a job.

00:21:23.000 --> 00:21:24.400
This is my hypothesis.

00:21:24.400 --> 00:21:27.850
I'm happy to have it critiqued.

00:21:27.850 --> 00:21:31.320
And you accepted a job
at the Ford Motor Company

00:21:31.320 --> 00:21:37.260
and went to Detroit
in, say, 1925.

00:21:37.260 --> 00:21:40.420
Still making the Model T.

00:21:40.420 --> 00:21:43.296
I think you would
expect, as an engineer,

00:21:43.296 --> 00:21:45.400
that you would be
asked to improve

00:21:45.400 --> 00:21:48.530
the functioning of
the assembly lines

00:21:48.530 --> 00:21:51.770
at River Rouge
and Highland Park.

00:21:51.770 --> 00:21:55.430
You would also be engaged
in some improvement

00:21:55.430 --> 00:22:01.260
in the model, Model
T. But you would

00:22:01.260 --> 00:22:08.370
expect these changes to be
accumulative as an engineer.

00:22:08.370 --> 00:22:11.580
I don't think you would expect
to be involved in projects that

00:22:11.580 --> 00:22:16.670
would bring about radical
changes in the assembly line

00:22:16.670 --> 00:22:19.840
or in the product.

00:22:19.840 --> 00:22:24.250
And also, you would
not expect a need

00:22:24.250 --> 00:22:28.840
to go back to MIT or
some other institution

00:22:28.840 --> 00:22:31.750
in order to keep abreast of
the discipline in which you

00:22:31.750 --> 00:22:33.510
were working.

00:22:33.510 --> 00:22:35.880
That's what I believe
a 1925 engineer would

00:22:35.880 --> 00:22:39.210
feel going to work he or she
at the Ford Motor Company,

00:22:39.210 --> 00:22:41.690
mostly hes then, of course.

00:22:41.690 --> 00:22:47.920
But today, one
finds that you may

00:22:47.920 --> 00:22:51.160
become involved in a project.

00:22:51.160 --> 00:22:54.550
And the project might bring
about a radical transformation

00:22:54.550 --> 00:22:58.490
in product, software,
or hardware,

00:22:58.490 --> 00:23:02.260
or a radical transformation
in the way the production

00:23:02.260 --> 00:23:04.420
line is functioning.

00:23:04.420 --> 00:23:10.740
And these projects remind me
in many ways of the theater.

00:23:10.740 --> 00:23:14.240
A friend of mine who is a
director and a producer,

00:23:14.240 --> 00:23:17.780
when I told her about these
projects and engineers

00:23:17.780 --> 00:23:19.400
are so often
involved in she said,

00:23:19.400 --> 00:23:25.460
oh, what you're telling
me about is open casting.

00:23:25.460 --> 00:23:28.510
And I asked her to
elaborate upon that.

00:23:28.510 --> 00:23:32.100
She said, all right,
we're doing a play.

00:23:32.100 --> 00:23:34.080
We're not a repertory company.

00:23:34.080 --> 00:23:35.700
We're doing a play.

00:23:35.700 --> 00:23:38.790
We announce open casting.

00:23:38.790 --> 00:23:43.630
And I know that companies like
Andersen and others do this.

00:23:43.630 --> 00:23:46.230
An announcement is made,
such and such a project

00:23:46.230 --> 00:23:48.120
will be put into place.

00:23:48.120 --> 00:23:52.020
The project might engage
you for two or three months

00:23:52.020 --> 00:23:54.530
or maybe even a year or more.

00:23:54.530 --> 00:23:57.710
And then after you
come join this project,

00:23:57.710 --> 00:24:00.110
and then when the project
fulfills its goal,

00:24:00.110 --> 00:24:01.850
then we will disperse.

00:24:01.850 --> 00:24:04.610
And you may become involved
in another project.

00:24:04.610 --> 00:24:06.689
In the case of the
theater, open casting,

00:24:06.689 --> 00:24:07.730
you make an announcement.

00:24:07.730 --> 00:24:10.940
We are doing "The
Iceman Cometh."

00:24:10.940 --> 00:24:13.670
People from all over
the Manhattan area

00:24:13.670 --> 00:24:16.190
will come and try out for parts.

00:24:16.190 --> 00:24:18.110
You put these people
together for a while.

00:24:18.110 --> 00:24:19.880
They rehearse for months.

00:24:19.880 --> 00:24:21.590
They put on a production.

00:24:21.590 --> 00:24:24.710
And after the run is
complete, they disperse.

00:24:24.710 --> 00:24:28.220
Incidentally, that
this open casting

00:24:28.220 --> 00:24:30.530
when you announce that you
are going to do a play,

00:24:30.530 --> 00:24:33.890
that's a cattle
call, incidentally.

00:24:33.890 --> 00:24:36.500
Now they also,
repertory companies,

00:24:36.500 --> 00:24:39.590
they do not have cattle
calls and open casting.

00:24:39.590 --> 00:24:43.240
But they have the
same people, as you

00:24:43.240 --> 00:24:46.490
know, doing different
projects with the same people.

00:24:46.490 --> 00:24:50.880
And you'll find both of these
forms in the industrial world,

00:24:50.880 --> 00:24:54.650
in the military world, and in
the university world today.

00:24:54.650 --> 00:24:58.950
Virtual projects as you know
they're sometimes called.

00:24:58.950 --> 00:25:06.520
And I will describe in a matter
of a few minutes, a project.

00:25:06.520 --> 00:25:11.956
One of the classic
projects of history.

00:25:11.956 --> 00:25:13.650
I think most of you
have heard of it.

00:25:13.650 --> 00:25:16.530
Perhaps some of you
have read about it.

00:25:16.530 --> 00:25:21.310
I note that people in the
Systems Design and Management,

00:25:21.310 --> 00:25:23.740
some of you are
interested in history.

00:25:23.740 --> 00:25:27.100
Well, this is one of the
great historical episodes.

00:25:27.100 --> 00:25:31.500
And that is the creation by
Thomas Edison and his group

00:25:31.500 --> 00:25:38.000
during the period from
about 1878 to 1882

00:25:38.000 --> 00:25:39.600
of an electric
light in and system.

00:25:42.270 --> 00:25:46.460
Now what I hope I will be able
to do in briefly describing

00:25:46.460 --> 00:25:50.230
this project is give
you what I consider

00:25:50.230 --> 00:25:57.190
to be the essence of
project, the project essence.

00:25:57.190 --> 00:25:59.220
And the projects you
may be involved in

00:25:59.220 --> 00:26:01.960
differ in detail, of course.

00:26:01.960 --> 00:26:05.200
And projects done throughout
history differ in many ways.

00:26:05.200 --> 00:26:09.460
But I am hoping to capture
the essence of a project

00:26:09.460 --> 00:26:13.310
by going through this
historical episode.

00:26:13.310 --> 00:26:18.980
First, program management, I am
contrasting program management

00:26:18.980 --> 00:26:21.560
and project management.

00:26:21.560 --> 00:26:26.730
I am drawing upon some NASA
policies in this regard.

00:26:26.730 --> 00:26:29.940
NASA, in the past
at least, and many

00:26:29.940 --> 00:26:32.730
of the things I say
come from the past,

00:26:32.730 --> 00:26:36.480
NASA defined program
management differently

00:26:36.480 --> 00:26:39.270
from project management.

00:26:39.270 --> 00:26:42.060
Now first, program management in
the case of the electric light

00:26:42.060 --> 00:26:44.820
and power system,
program management,

00:26:44.820 --> 00:26:49.300
people involved in this
are concerned about funding

00:26:49.300 --> 00:26:51.830
and are concerned
about politics.

00:26:51.830 --> 00:26:55.390
And sometimes, they
must become involved

00:26:55.390 --> 00:26:59.160
in organizational
creation as well.

00:26:59.160 --> 00:27:01.810
Now in the case of this
particular project,

00:27:01.810 --> 00:27:04.040
an organization had been formed.

00:27:04.040 --> 00:27:05.800
And as I go through
this, perhaps you'll

00:27:05.800 --> 00:27:10.300
be impressed, as I am
every time I involve myself

00:27:10.300 --> 00:27:12.070
with the history of
this project, how

00:27:12.070 --> 00:27:18.280
contemporary this Edison
story is in its detail.

00:27:18.280 --> 00:27:20.620
Perhaps you will agree with me.

00:27:20.620 --> 00:27:22.400
Perhaps not.

00:27:22.400 --> 00:27:26.990
But a company was formed 1878
called the Edison Electric

00:27:26.990 --> 00:27:28.790
Light Company.

00:27:28.790 --> 00:27:30.410
Now the Edison
Electric Light Company

00:27:30.410 --> 00:27:34.430
was formed by the
program manager types.

00:27:34.430 --> 00:27:37.190
One of these was a man
named Grosvenor Lowrey,

00:27:37.190 --> 00:27:42.020
who was a very prominent
New York City lawyer.

00:27:42.020 --> 00:27:44.720
And it's not unusual
to find people

00:27:44.720 --> 00:27:48.230
from law and other disciplines
involved with engineers

00:27:48.230 --> 00:27:51.220
in these open system creations.

00:27:51.220 --> 00:27:55.430
Now Grosvenor Lowrey was the
lawyer for the Western Union

00:27:55.430 --> 00:27:56.390
Telegraph company.

00:27:56.390 --> 00:27:59.620
So he was working in
the world of technology.

00:27:59.620 --> 00:28:04.320
Lowrey said, I think we should
create an electric light

00:28:04.320 --> 00:28:04.820
system.

00:28:04.820 --> 00:28:07.120
I'll explain why he chose
that problem in a moment.

00:28:07.120 --> 00:28:08.310
He said this to Edison.

00:28:08.310 --> 00:28:10.730
He said, first, we must
create this company.

00:28:10.730 --> 00:28:14.030
And this company will be an
investment and patent holding

00:28:14.030 --> 00:28:15.170
company.

00:28:15.170 --> 00:28:17.460
What's new?

00:28:17.460 --> 00:28:20.130
It will fund
Edison's activities.

00:28:20.130 --> 00:28:22.140
It will take over his patents.

00:28:22.140 --> 00:28:24.480
It will get a return
on the investment.

00:28:24.480 --> 00:28:28.030
This is venture capital in 1878.

00:28:28.030 --> 00:28:29.850
And the company was formed.

00:28:29.850 --> 00:28:34.920
Another thing Lowrey did, and
this is another requirement

00:28:34.920 --> 00:28:37.620
of open systems, as
some of you here I know

00:28:37.620 --> 00:28:42.420
are familiar with this,
the political stage

00:28:42.420 --> 00:28:46.050
has to be set for the project.

00:28:46.050 --> 00:28:49.590
And many engineers
in projects are not

00:28:49.590 --> 00:28:52.620
aware of the
political maneuvering

00:28:52.620 --> 00:28:54.960
that has gone on before the
project comes into being.

00:28:54.960 --> 00:28:59.670
In this case, the councilmen
in the city of New York in 1878

00:28:59.670 --> 00:29:01.860
were very much
under the influence

00:29:01.860 --> 00:29:05.460
of the gas lighting people.

00:29:05.460 --> 00:29:07.980
And so the gas lighting
people put a lot of pressure

00:29:07.980 --> 00:29:10.470
on the councilmen in
the city of New York

00:29:10.470 --> 00:29:12.480
when the gas lighting
people learned

00:29:12.480 --> 00:29:18.150
that there was to be an effort
to create an electric lighting

00:29:18.150 --> 00:29:19.440
system.

00:29:19.440 --> 00:29:21.390
And the gas people
said to the councilmen,

00:29:21.390 --> 00:29:25.730
don't let these people
open up the streets,

00:29:25.730 --> 00:29:30.590
because the distribution
lines would be put underground

00:29:30.590 --> 00:29:31.760
in New York City.

00:29:31.760 --> 00:29:34.400
So one way to block
the project was not

00:29:34.400 --> 00:29:36.830
to give the Edison
Electric Light Company

00:29:36.830 --> 00:29:38.610
permission to open the streets.

00:29:38.610 --> 00:29:42.700
Well, Grosvenor Lowrey went to
work lobbying and in other ways

00:29:42.700 --> 00:29:45.890
in bringing about a
change in attitude

00:29:45.890 --> 00:29:49.220
on the part of the
councilmen of New York City.

00:29:49.220 --> 00:29:51.440
And I think you can well
imagine what he might have

00:29:51.440 --> 00:29:54.570
done to change their minds.

00:29:54.570 --> 00:29:57.990
So the political stage was set.

00:29:57.990 --> 00:30:01.280
And very quickly, I don't
want to dwell on this,

00:30:01.280 --> 00:30:04.460
but it's an anecdote.

00:30:04.460 --> 00:30:07.430
One thing Larry did, he
invited all the councilmen out

00:30:07.430 --> 00:30:13.280
to see a test of the Edison
electric light system in 1879.

00:30:13.280 --> 00:30:14.990
And he was taking the--

00:30:14.990 --> 00:30:19.130
he brought them out in
a handsome railway car,

00:30:19.130 --> 00:30:22.940
took them around and showed
them a proto electric light

00:30:22.940 --> 00:30:29.580
system that Edison had put up
at his Menlo Park laboratories.

00:30:29.580 --> 00:30:32.290
And these people were
becoming very bored,

00:30:32.290 --> 00:30:34.610
the councilmen, with
what they were seeing.

00:30:34.610 --> 00:30:37.850
And then the councilmen were
moved into Edison's laboratory.

00:30:37.850 --> 00:30:39.830
It was completely dark.

00:30:39.830 --> 00:30:42.080
Suddenly, the lights came on.

00:30:42.080 --> 00:30:45.240
The first display of indoor
lighting for these people.

00:30:45.240 --> 00:30:47.420
And when the lights came
on, what was displayed?

00:30:47.420 --> 00:30:50.480
A marvelous meal
from Delmonico's

00:30:50.480 --> 00:30:54.330
with much liquid refreshment
to go along with it.

00:30:54.330 --> 00:30:56.480
And incidentally,
that banquet had

00:30:56.480 --> 00:30:59.630
to end within an
hour and 35 minutes

00:30:59.630 --> 00:31:04.010
because Edison's electric
lights would not burn for longer

00:31:04.010 --> 00:31:06.050
than an hour and 35 minutes.

00:31:06.050 --> 00:31:09.600
Now this is program management.

00:31:09.600 --> 00:31:10.730
We have to respect it.

00:31:10.730 --> 00:31:12.290
It sets the stage.

00:31:12.290 --> 00:31:17.140
Now we go into Edison
as the project manager.

00:31:17.140 --> 00:31:22.270
Now let's forget the
heroic Edison of the books

00:31:22.270 --> 00:31:24.670
we read when we were young.

00:31:24.670 --> 00:31:28.150
This man was not working
alone in a garret.

00:31:28.150 --> 00:31:31.430
He had one of the best equipped
laboratories in the United

00:31:31.430 --> 00:31:33.280
States.

00:31:33.280 --> 00:31:35.650
He also had his team.

00:31:35.650 --> 00:31:40.390
His team included a physicist,
who had studied in Berlin.

00:31:40.390 --> 00:31:45.960
It included marvelously
talented machine tool people.

00:31:45.960 --> 00:31:48.950
There were also
chemists in his team

00:31:48.950 --> 00:31:51.430
as well as glass
blowers who were brought

00:31:51.430 --> 00:31:52.710
into it for this project.

00:31:52.710 --> 00:31:54.670
I could go on and
name the craftsmen.

00:31:54.670 --> 00:31:58.270
But the point to be made
here that the historian wants

00:31:58.270 --> 00:32:03.070
to make, Edison had brought
this group, this team together.

00:32:03.070 --> 00:32:05.620
And I know many of you have
been involved in teams.

00:32:05.620 --> 00:32:07.780
Edison had brought
this team together

00:32:07.780 --> 00:32:10.540
in earlier years to
work on the telephone

00:32:10.540 --> 00:32:12.190
and to work on the phonograph.

00:32:12.190 --> 00:32:17.720
Now he had this team in place,
so he had to choose a product.

00:32:17.720 --> 00:32:19.960
He had to choose a
system that could

00:32:19.960 --> 00:32:22.740
utilize the resources
in the personnel

00:32:22.740 --> 00:32:25.240
that he had at his disposal.

00:32:25.240 --> 00:32:28.180
So I think we need
to remind ourselves

00:32:28.180 --> 00:32:32.590
that we are constrained by the
organizational concepts that

00:32:32.590 --> 00:32:36.250
come out of the past and in
which we are working now.

00:32:36.250 --> 00:32:37.520
We are constrained.

00:32:37.520 --> 00:32:39.280
There are only certain
projects we'll do.

00:32:39.280 --> 00:32:42.890
There are only certain
projects that are suitable.

00:32:42.890 --> 00:32:45.890
So Edison decided
that electric lighting

00:32:45.890 --> 00:32:49.430
was suitable for the team
that he had in place.

00:32:49.430 --> 00:32:53.690
Now once he had
his team in place

00:32:53.690 --> 00:32:56.000
and he had his
equipment in place,

00:32:56.000 --> 00:33:01.130
then there was the
problem of conceptualizing

00:33:01.130 --> 00:33:03.560
the electric light system.

00:33:03.560 --> 00:33:06.260
And I think this is
where Edison's brilliance

00:33:06.260 --> 00:33:10.310
along with the intelligence
of some of his team

00:33:10.310 --> 00:33:12.030
comes into play.

00:33:12.030 --> 00:33:15.320
And if I may have
the other diagram.

00:33:15.320 --> 00:33:16.020
Yes.

00:33:16.020 --> 00:33:18.920
There we are.

00:33:18.920 --> 00:33:23.190
There's nothing in
Edison's notebooks

00:33:23.190 --> 00:33:28.830
that gives me the
evidence to feel

00:33:28.830 --> 00:33:31.830
confident in saying that
what I'm telling you now

00:33:31.830 --> 00:33:36.290
is other than a hypothesis.

00:33:36.290 --> 00:33:39.390
But from circumstantial
evidence,

00:33:39.390 --> 00:33:43.220
this is the way that
I believe Edison

00:33:43.220 --> 00:33:51.160
reasoned his way to his
concept, his design concept.

00:33:51.160 --> 00:33:53.800
As you know in a
project, a design concept

00:33:53.800 --> 00:33:56.640
comes before a
preliminary design.

00:33:56.640 --> 00:34:01.830
Well, from this little
exercise in following

00:34:01.830 --> 00:34:05.130
Edison's reasoning, I
think you may better

00:34:05.130 --> 00:34:08.100
understand the man's
so-called and I

00:34:08.100 --> 00:34:11.219
would say, yes, his genius.

00:34:11.219 --> 00:34:15.239
You'll note the
interdisciplinary character

00:34:15.239 --> 00:34:18.159
of the thought processes.

00:34:18.159 --> 00:34:22.540
He realized he had to analyze
the market and with whom was he

00:34:22.540 --> 00:34:24.380
competing.

00:34:24.380 --> 00:34:27.699
He was competing
with gas lighting.

00:34:27.699 --> 00:34:32.139
And from gas lighting, he
drew an analogy with regard

00:34:32.139 --> 00:34:35.199
to the overall
architecture of the system

00:34:35.199 --> 00:34:37.150
that he would design.

00:34:37.150 --> 00:34:40.639
He had to be concerned about
the economics of competing,

00:34:40.639 --> 00:34:43.674
but also this analogy
with the architecture.

00:34:43.674 --> 00:34:45.070
It was very helpful to him.

00:34:45.070 --> 00:34:47.980
Gas lighting or
the gas companies

00:34:47.980 --> 00:34:51.026
were then distributing
from central plants.

00:34:51.026 --> 00:34:52.900
They were distributing
through an entire city

00:34:52.900 --> 00:34:54.550
from one central plant.

00:34:54.550 --> 00:34:57.400
And Edison decided that
his architecture would

00:34:57.400 --> 00:34:58.810
be like that architecture.

00:34:58.810 --> 00:35:01.870
But let's go back to the
economics of the system.

00:35:04.420 --> 00:35:07.930
He had a general concept of
what the system would be like.

00:35:07.930 --> 00:35:10.720
He would have distribution
lines of copper.

00:35:10.720 --> 00:35:12.640
It would have
generators, of course.

00:35:12.640 --> 00:35:15.160
And it would have
the electric lights.

00:35:15.160 --> 00:35:18.790
And he reasoned with the
help of some of probably

00:35:18.790 --> 00:35:20.470
with Upton, the
man who'd studied

00:35:20.470 --> 00:35:24.520
in Berlin under
Helmholtz, he reasoned

00:35:24.520 --> 00:35:27.790
that the reverse salient in the
system, the bottleneck, if you

00:35:27.790 --> 00:35:30.200
prefer, the reverse
salient in the system

00:35:30.200 --> 00:35:33.060
was the cost of copper.

00:35:33.060 --> 00:35:36.530
So here's a man who is into
designing an electrical system

00:35:36.530 --> 00:35:39.860
but he realized that
materials are his problem.

00:35:39.860 --> 00:35:42.680
And Edison didn't
hesitate to move

00:35:42.680 --> 00:35:45.890
from one area from materials
area into the electrical area

00:35:45.890 --> 00:35:47.400
into the chemical area.

00:35:47.400 --> 00:35:50.490
Wherever the problem
was, he would move.

00:35:50.490 --> 00:35:53.880
He wasn't bound by any
disciplinary restraints.

00:35:53.880 --> 00:35:56.780
So he said, the problem
is the cost of copper,

00:35:56.780 --> 00:36:00.350
the cost of copper in
the distribution lines.

00:36:00.350 --> 00:36:06.410
Then he turns from materials
and cost to the science

00:36:06.410 --> 00:36:08.670
that he had at his disposal.

00:36:08.670 --> 00:36:13.070
Now remember this is
1878 and the science

00:36:13.070 --> 00:36:15.480
available to electricians
is very limited.

00:36:15.480 --> 00:36:19.250
He had Joule's Law
and he had Ohm's Law.

00:36:19.250 --> 00:36:21.860
And from thinking
with Joule's Law

00:36:21.860 --> 00:36:24.800
and thinking with
Ohm's Law, he knew

00:36:24.800 --> 00:36:28.670
that the losses in
distribution would

00:36:28.670 --> 00:36:34.520
be an equal to the square of the
current times the resistance.

00:36:34.520 --> 00:36:38.420
Or to put it another way, the
energy loss in the conductors

00:36:38.420 --> 00:36:41.540
would be proportional to
the current squared times

00:36:41.540 --> 00:36:44.390
the length of the
conductor divided

00:36:44.390 --> 00:36:46.700
by the cross sectional area.

00:36:46.700 --> 00:36:48.920
If one increased the
cross sectional area

00:36:48.920 --> 00:36:51.110
of the distribution
lines, then one

00:36:51.110 --> 00:36:54.650
would reduce the energy loss
in the distribution lines.

00:36:54.650 --> 00:36:56.780
But to increase the
cross sectional area

00:36:56.780 --> 00:36:58.970
was to increase
the use of copper.

00:36:58.970 --> 00:37:02.630
Therefore, this was
not an option for him

00:37:02.630 --> 00:37:07.520
to continue to increase the
cross sectional area until he

00:37:07.520 --> 00:37:10.940
lowered his energy
lost in distribution.

00:37:10.940 --> 00:37:14.720
So assuming a fixed length and
keeping the cross section area

00:37:14.720 --> 00:37:20.330
low, how was he to
solve the problem

00:37:20.330 --> 00:37:24.940
of getting the required
amount of energy

00:37:24.940 --> 00:37:28.280
into his electric lights?

00:37:28.280 --> 00:37:32.050
Now he knew from Ohm's Law
that the electric lights

00:37:32.050 --> 00:37:36.770
had to have a power
on energy consumption

00:37:36.770 --> 00:37:39.830
which was equal to the
current times the voltage.

00:37:39.830 --> 00:37:43.660
Now he knew from the energy
loss in the distribution lines,

00:37:43.660 --> 00:37:47.260
he had to keep his current
low, because the energy loss

00:37:47.260 --> 00:37:48.400
was current square.

00:37:48.400 --> 00:37:51.130
I'm going across into
this in such detail,

00:37:51.130 --> 00:37:52.990
it may seem
elementary, but this is

00:37:52.990 --> 00:37:56.950
the way I think the
man's mind was working.

00:37:56.950 --> 00:38:01.750
And so therefore, he has
to keep the current low

00:38:01.750 --> 00:38:03.130
because current square.

00:38:03.130 --> 00:38:05.530
He'll raise the
voltage, so he will

00:38:05.530 --> 00:38:08.680
have sufficient current
times voltage to give him

00:38:08.680 --> 00:38:13.410
the energy he needs
at his electric lamps.

00:38:13.410 --> 00:38:18.220
Now how does he raise the
voltage and lower the current?

00:38:18.220 --> 00:38:21.970
I went into an old Encyclopedia
Britannica for that period.

00:38:21.970 --> 00:38:25.870
And I found that the way
that resistance, Ohm's Law

00:38:25.870 --> 00:38:31.150
was stated in those days was
that the resistance equals

00:38:31.150 --> 00:38:33.550
the ratio of voltage to current.

00:38:33.550 --> 00:38:35.240
And there's the obvious answer.

00:38:35.240 --> 00:38:38.710
I think that's the
eureka moment for Edison

00:38:38.710 --> 00:38:41.380
was when he saw that by
raising the resistance

00:38:41.380 --> 00:38:44.260
of the electric light
element, he could increase

00:38:44.260 --> 00:38:47.110
the voltage that would be
going through his transmission

00:38:47.110 --> 00:38:50.720
distribution lines and
reduce the current.

00:38:50.720 --> 00:38:55.620
So that is why the man, and we
go into popular stories now,

00:38:55.620 --> 00:38:59.420
spent months trying to
find a light filament that

00:38:59.420 --> 00:39:02.540
gave a maximum of resistance.

00:39:02.540 --> 00:39:07.580
And what he ended up with and
that determines even today

00:39:07.580 --> 00:39:11.780
the standards of our
electric lighting system,

00:39:11.780 --> 00:39:16.250
he found that he could
get a filament that

00:39:16.250 --> 00:39:23.030
would take a current of
one amp, that it would

00:39:23.030 --> 00:39:26.480
have a resistance of 100 ohms.

00:39:26.480 --> 00:39:29.330
And so that would
give him 100 watts.

00:39:29.330 --> 00:39:31.640
And so there you
have the standards

00:39:31.640 --> 00:39:34.040
that we have in our
electric lights today

00:39:34.040 --> 00:39:38.600
because the highest resistance
he could get was 100 ohms.

00:39:38.600 --> 00:39:41.680
He needed one amp to get
the wattage he needed.

00:39:41.680 --> 00:39:43.580
Why that wattage of 100 watts?

00:39:43.580 --> 00:39:46.310
Because 100 watts gave
him the same lighting

00:39:46.310 --> 00:39:54.610
intensity as a gas light was
giving at that particular time.

00:39:54.610 --> 00:39:58.540
So Edison's conceptual design.

00:39:58.540 --> 00:40:05.040
I hope that you are as impressed
by the man's thinking process

00:40:05.040 --> 00:40:10.145
as much as I am, even though
the scientific laws are not

00:40:10.145 --> 00:40:10.645
esoteric.

00:40:10.645 --> 00:40:14.220
The quantitative activity
is not remarkable.

00:40:14.220 --> 00:40:19.160
But putting together the
various aspects of the problem

00:40:19.160 --> 00:40:22.370
is what I find
extremely impressive.

00:40:22.370 --> 00:40:25.300
Now let's go on to
the open system.

00:40:25.300 --> 00:40:30.370
He now has the essence of his
electric technical system,

00:40:30.370 --> 00:40:31.630
his closed system.

00:40:31.630 --> 00:40:34.116
Now we go into the organization
of the Edison company.

00:40:42.690 --> 00:40:46.790
I have interviewed
a number of persons

00:40:46.790 --> 00:40:51.690
in the field of complex systems.

00:40:51.690 --> 00:40:55.810
And they have told me
that it's necessary

00:40:55.810 --> 00:41:03.150
if the closed system is radical,
if the technology is radical,

00:41:03.150 --> 00:41:10.060
it is advantageous to create
a new organization to nurture

00:41:10.060 --> 00:41:12.619
the radical technology.

00:41:12.619 --> 00:41:14.660
They tell me, and I'm
thinking about an interview

00:41:14.660 --> 00:41:19.362
with Simon Ramo, who worked
on the Atlas missile,

00:41:19.362 --> 00:41:22.330
did systems engineering
for the Atlas missile.

00:41:22.330 --> 00:41:25.210
And I'm also thinking about
General Schriever, who presided

00:41:25.210 --> 00:41:26.294
over the Atlas missile.

00:41:26.294 --> 00:41:28.210
And I'm thinking about
the people who presided

00:41:28.210 --> 00:41:30.280
over the Polaris missile.

00:41:30.280 --> 00:41:32.200
And in both those
cases, the Polaris

00:41:32.200 --> 00:41:37.630
and the Atlas and numerous
other system designs,

00:41:37.630 --> 00:41:43.750
the system builders not only
produce the new technology,

00:41:43.750 --> 00:41:46.090
but produce new
organizations in order

00:41:46.090 --> 00:41:49.420
to get the radical
technology outside

00:41:49.420 --> 00:41:52.362
of the bureaucratic structure.

00:41:52.362 --> 00:41:53.820
General Schriever
told me, and I'll

00:41:53.820 --> 00:41:56.510
talk about Atlas next
time, General Schriever

00:41:56.510 --> 00:42:01.730
said, I had to get out,
away from those bastards

00:42:01.730 --> 00:42:03.530
at the Pentagon.

00:42:03.530 --> 00:42:06.650
He said, I was dealing with
people who could say, no,

00:42:06.650 --> 00:42:09.790
and could not say, yes,
the assistant secretaries.

00:42:09.790 --> 00:42:12.300
I had to get outside the system.

00:42:12.300 --> 00:42:15.200
So he forms a new organization,
the Western Development

00:42:15.200 --> 00:42:16.760
Corporation, part
of the Air Force,

00:42:16.760 --> 00:42:20.150
but outside of the Air
Force and the Pentagon

00:42:20.150 --> 00:42:21.160
bureaucratic structure.

00:42:21.160 --> 00:42:23.800
Well, look what Edison did.

00:42:23.800 --> 00:42:28.150
Edison and his advisors did
not depend upon existing

00:42:28.150 --> 00:42:30.130
manufacturing companies.

00:42:30.130 --> 00:42:33.310
But they formed this
consortium of companies

00:42:33.310 --> 00:42:38.440
in which the parent Edison
company, the Edison Electric

00:42:38.440 --> 00:42:41.860
Light Company, the venture
capital patent holding company

00:42:41.860 --> 00:42:45.430
had interests through
stock, in most cases.

00:42:45.430 --> 00:42:46.930
So you had the
Illuminating Company,

00:42:46.930 --> 00:42:50.110
which is a utility, the
Edison Electric Illuminating

00:42:50.110 --> 00:42:52.070
Company in New
York and Brooklyn,

00:42:52.070 --> 00:42:54.160
and other illuminating
companies,

00:42:54.160 --> 00:42:56.560
the Thomas Edison
Construction Company

00:42:56.560 --> 00:43:00.520
to construct the electric light
and power systems, the Edison

00:43:00.520 --> 00:43:05.590
Machine Works that manufactured
dynamos, the Edison Tube

00:43:05.590 --> 00:43:08.470
Company, underground
distribution, lamp

00:43:08.470 --> 00:43:09.790
works obvious.

00:43:09.790 --> 00:43:13.210
Bergman was fixtures,
and other manufacturing

00:43:13.210 --> 00:43:14.830
came from United Edison.

00:43:14.830 --> 00:43:19.780
What it is worth noting here,
this system builder, Edison

00:43:19.780 --> 00:43:25.540
and his team, they have
control of the manufacturer,

00:43:25.540 --> 00:43:28.194
like the German General Electric
I told you a moment ago,

00:43:28.194 --> 00:43:29.735
they're controlling
the manufacturer.

00:43:29.735 --> 00:43:33.940
And they're controlling the
market for the manufacturer.

00:43:33.940 --> 00:43:35.980
That is, they're
controlling the utilities

00:43:35.980 --> 00:43:39.490
that buy the generators that
are made by the Edison machine

00:43:39.490 --> 00:43:41.050
works.

00:43:41.050 --> 00:43:43.540
What's new Mr. Gates?

00:43:43.540 --> 00:43:46.630
In this effort to get control
not only of other production,

00:43:46.630 --> 00:43:50.640
but the content and
distribution and so forth.

00:43:50.640 --> 00:43:54.840
And so we see this control
at a very, very early day.

00:43:54.840 --> 00:43:57.600
Incidentally, most of the
manufacturing companies

00:43:57.600 --> 00:44:00.390
here were combined
in the early 1890s

00:44:00.390 --> 00:44:04.410
into the General
Electric Company.

00:44:04.410 --> 00:44:08.100
Now I want to turn
to a more recent--

00:44:08.100 --> 00:44:11.670
well, I should say
that the system

00:44:11.670 --> 00:44:15.690
was deployed in New York,
in Brooklyn, and elsewhere.

00:44:15.690 --> 00:44:20.190
And the company, the
Consolidated Edison Company

00:44:20.190 --> 00:44:21.480
flourishes in New York.

00:44:21.480 --> 00:44:25.540
It was established by
Edison those many years ago,

00:44:25.540 --> 00:44:26.977
et cetera, et cetera, et cetera.

00:44:26.977 --> 00:44:29.310
I don't think we need to go
into the further development

00:44:29.310 --> 00:44:30.050
and deployment.

00:44:30.050 --> 00:44:34.650
Let's now turn to
the ARPANET, which

00:44:34.650 --> 00:44:39.660
I think you all know is the
beginning or the predecessor

00:44:39.660 --> 00:44:40.500
of the internet.

00:44:43.810 --> 00:44:49.360
And this story is I think one
of the remarkable accounts

00:44:49.360 --> 00:44:55.360
of system building
in the last 30 years.

00:44:55.360 --> 00:45:01.540
So I would spend some time
telling the story of ARPANET.

00:45:01.540 --> 00:45:06.600
Again, I mean to
say telling a story.

00:45:06.600 --> 00:45:09.270
Because I know most
of you are involved

00:45:09.270 --> 00:45:12.440
in very elegant problem solving.

00:45:12.440 --> 00:45:17.300
Your reports are rigorous
and quantitative.

00:45:17.300 --> 00:45:20.840
And I'm telling a story,
which you usually do not

00:45:20.840 --> 00:45:23.090
do in your professional work.

00:45:23.090 --> 00:45:27.072
The reason I'm telling a
story is because stories can--

00:45:27.072 --> 00:45:28.280
well, there are many reasons.

00:45:28.280 --> 00:45:30.380
I couldn't do the kind
of work you're doing.

00:45:30.380 --> 00:45:37.240
But another reason is stories
encompass messy complexity

00:45:37.240 --> 00:45:39.910
in a more embracing
inclusive way

00:45:39.910 --> 00:45:45.220
than a more scientific
elegant account can do.

00:45:45.220 --> 00:45:49.600
So a story is one way
of conveying messiness.

00:45:49.600 --> 00:45:54.400
And I'm arguing and I'm arguing
with some of my friends at MIT

00:45:54.400 --> 00:45:57.100
that MIT students
need to hear more

00:45:57.100 --> 00:46:00.070
stories of messy complexity.

00:46:00.070 --> 00:46:01.930
They may find it soft.

00:46:01.930 --> 00:46:05.590
And they may find it
at first not congenial.

00:46:05.590 --> 00:46:08.650
But the people I've talked
to out in the field often

00:46:08.650 --> 00:46:13.630
say to me, yes, yes, do tell
messy stories because the mess

00:46:13.630 --> 00:46:15.790
is what many of
the young engineers

00:46:15.790 --> 00:46:18.100
will soon be involved with.

00:46:18.100 --> 00:46:20.640
All right, turning
to the ARPANET.

00:46:20.640 --> 00:46:25.630
In following the pattern
that I have described

00:46:25.630 --> 00:46:30.190
in the case of the
classic Edison story,

00:46:30.190 --> 00:46:34.920
let's begin with program
management of the ARPANET.

00:46:34.920 --> 00:46:38.550
Now the program manager
of the ARPANET, this

00:46:38.550 --> 00:46:41.220
is my terminology, some
people in the audience

00:46:41.220 --> 00:46:45.500
may disagree, the
program management was

00:46:45.500 --> 00:46:50.540
the responsibility of DARPA,
the Defense Advanced Research

00:46:50.540 --> 00:46:52.610
Projects Agency.

00:46:52.610 --> 00:46:55.340
And I trust most of you
are familiar with it.

00:46:55.340 --> 00:47:01.190
It has been a remarkably
successful funder and nourisher

00:47:01.190 --> 00:47:06.910
of projects since 1958.

00:47:06.910 --> 00:47:08.380
It is still flourishing.

00:47:08.380 --> 00:47:13.990
It has changed in character,
in policy, but it flourishes.

00:47:13.990 --> 00:47:16.720
It has been called at times
Advanced Research Projects

00:47:16.720 --> 00:47:18.520
Agency, ARPA.

00:47:18.520 --> 00:47:22.540
At other times, the Defense
Advanced Research Projects

00:47:22.540 --> 00:47:23.620
Agency.

00:47:23.620 --> 00:47:26.170
I refer to it as DARPA.

00:47:26.170 --> 00:47:30.010
It was founded, established
in 1958 in response

00:47:30.010 --> 00:47:32.900
to the Sputnik.

00:47:32.900 --> 00:47:35.840
If you don't remember,
I do remember.

00:47:35.840 --> 00:47:40.820
You may have heard that after
the Russians launched Sputnik,

00:47:40.820 --> 00:47:42.740
there was great
anxiety in this country

00:47:42.740 --> 00:47:46.040
that our technology
was falling behind

00:47:46.040 --> 00:47:50.150
and there were some measures
had to be taken the Eisenhower

00:47:50.150 --> 00:47:55.230
administration felt in order
to respond to this problem.

00:47:55.230 --> 00:47:59.050
And one of the
responses was DARPA,

00:47:59.050 --> 00:48:03.835
which was to nourish and develop
and fund radical technology.

00:48:03.835 --> 00:48:06.490
I'm using radical in
a very general way,

00:48:06.490 --> 00:48:09.460
but highly innovative
technology, if you prefer.

00:48:09.460 --> 00:48:15.900
Now note that DARPA
is a Pentagon agency.

00:48:15.900 --> 00:48:18.700
DARPA gives military funding.

00:48:18.700 --> 00:48:23.050
But I stress that because DARPA
has funded a number of projects

00:48:23.050 --> 00:48:28.480
that in their impact and even
in the process of being created

00:48:28.480 --> 00:48:32.410
seem rather distant
from military needs.

00:48:32.410 --> 00:48:35.920
But let me go on with
the project management

00:48:35.920 --> 00:48:45.200
that DARPA was engaged in
when the organization decided

00:48:45.200 --> 00:48:47.670
to do a computer network.

00:48:47.670 --> 00:48:51.340
All right, now first, what's
momentarily distracting me,

00:48:51.340 --> 00:48:54.770
I see a quote I want
to give you here.

00:48:54.770 --> 00:48:59.470
One of the major DARPA
personalities, Licklider,

00:48:59.470 --> 00:49:05.030
said, what the military needs is
what the business main needs is

00:49:05.030 --> 00:49:07.250
what the scientist needs.

00:49:07.250 --> 00:49:12.420
So what the military funds
can be useful of advantage

00:49:12.420 --> 00:49:15.210
to businessmen and scientists.

00:49:15.210 --> 00:49:17.040
And this statement
can be made as long

00:49:17.040 --> 00:49:19.730
as the level of
abstraction is high.

00:49:19.730 --> 00:49:22.100
Where the businessman and the
scientist and the military

00:49:22.100 --> 00:49:26.150
find their needs differ is
when one lowers the project

00:49:26.150 --> 00:49:29.510
down the ladder of abstraction
to very practical, specific

00:49:29.510 --> 00:49:30.720
applications.

00:49:30.720 --> 00:49:33.680
But when working at a general
level, a computer network,

00:49:33.680 --> 00:49:37.250
general, all of
these contingencies,

00:49:37.250 --> 00:49:41.540
these clients can be
responded to at this level.

00:49:41.540 --> 00:49:44.360
Another person I
interviewed told me

00:49:44.360 --> 00:49:47.630
that the military is
a very big society.

00:49:47.630 --> 00:49:49.670
The United States is
a very big society.

00:49:49.670 --> 00:49:51.740
This is a DARPA person.

00:49:51.740 --> 00:49:55.010
So is it any
surprise when we fund

00:49:55.010 --> 00:50:01.040
a military society need we also
funding a civil society needs?

00:50:01.040 --> 00:50:02.960
Both, for example,
need communications.

00:50:02.960 --> 00:50:07.080
Both need calculations,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:50:07.080 --> 00:50:11.370
All right, another
characteristic

00:50:11.370 --> 00:50:18.960
of this program manager,
DARPA, besides its willingness

00:50:18.960 --> 00:50:22.110
to innovate on a
rather abstract level

00:50:22.110 --> 00:50:25.740
and respond to a
number of interest

00:50:25.740 --> 00:50:28.920
was that the people
in the early days who

00:50:28.920 --> 00:50:34.220
came into DARPA to manage
as program managers,

00:50:34.220 --> 00:50:39.740
they were often on leave for two
or three years from industry,

00:50:39.740 --> 00:50:43.626
on leave from universities
for two or three years.

00:50:43.626 --> 00:50:44.750
So what were they bringing?

00:50:44.750 --> 00:50:48.380
They were bringing with them a
university style or an industry

00:50:48.380 --> 00:50:49.820
research style.

00:50:49.820 --> 00:50:50.900
What else?

00:50:50.900 --> 00:50:56.240
Two or three years,
and then they left.

00:50:56.240 --> 00:50:59.570
The project was
complete for them.

00:50:59.570 --> 00:51:01.970
Their performance was over.

00:51:01.970 --> 00:51:05.030
They left and others came in.

00:51:05.030 --> 00:51:07.970
It is difficult to
argue that DARPA

00:51:07.970 --> 00:51:13.040
was a bureaucracy with hardening
of the arteries when you have

00:51:13.040 --> 00:51:16.850
these people coming in for
short periods with new ideas

00:51:16.850 --> 00:51:20.310
soon to be replaced by
other people with new ideas.

00:51:20.310 --> 00:51:23.650
Another characteristic of
DARPA, the program management,

00:51:23.650 --> 00:51:29.530
the staff was extremely
lean, relatively few people

00:51:29.530 --> 00:51:33.190
compared to other agencies.

00:51:33.190 --> 00:51:36.010
And another characteristic
was that DARPA

00:51:36.010 --> 00:51:41.320
preferred to fund very
large expensive projects.

00:51:41.320 --> 00:51:44.500
And this meant that a
small staff could manage,

00:51:44.500 --> 00:51:47.620
instead of having to manage
1,000 little projects.

00:51:47.620 --> 00:51:50.020
You had a small staff,
five or six people

00:51:50.020 --> 00:51:52.090
in case of one of the
branches of DARPA,

00:51:52.090 --> 00:51:54.330
that is IPTO, which I'll
talk about in a minute.

00:51:54.330 --> 00:51:57.280
You have five or six people
managing millions of dollars,

00:51:57.280 --> 00:51:59.920
but only a few projects.

00:51:59.920 --> 00:52:02.580
Now I want to say something
about a remarkable project

00:52:02.580 --> 00:52:03.840
manager.

00:52:03.840 --> 00:52:05.910
I'm sorry.

00:52:05.910 --> 00:52:07.720
I've been talking about
program management.

00:52:07.720 --> 00:52:09.136
Now I want to talk
about a project

00:52:09.136 --> 00:52:15.310
manager, a person who worked
within the context of DARPA.

00:52:15.310 --> 00:52:18.430
And that is Joseph Carl
Robnett Licklider, who

00:52:18.430 --> 00:52:22.270
is usually called Licklider.

00:52:22.270 --> 00:52:26.650
He set a pattern of
project management

00:52:26.650 --> 00:52:29.500
that I think has had
enormous influence

00:52:29.500 --> 00:52:33.370
in the world of project
management and engineering

00:52:33.370 --> 00:52:35.290
to this very day.

00:52:35.290 --> 00:52:37.900
He had a number of
devoted followers

00:52:37.900 --> 00:52:42.160
who admired his style,
saw him as a visionary,

00:52:42.160 --> 00:52:49.870
and put into place and deployed
the kind of project management

00:52:49.870 --> 00:52:53.260
style for which he became noted.

00:52:53.260 --> 00:52:59.450
Let me say something about
Licklider in the time I have.

00:52:59.450 --> 00:53:02.430
Note the man's pedigree.

00:53:02.430 --> 00:53:06.810
This gives us insight into
a very interesting period

00:53:06.810 --> 00:53:11.980
of his history and some
fascinating people.

00:53:11.980 --> 00:53:15.960
Licklider was a
psychologist by training.

00:53:15.960 --> 00:53:19.050
I should say here that
he was the first head

00:53:19.050 --> 00:53:22.320
of the information
processing techniques office,

00:53:22.320 --> 00:53:27.780
IPTO, which was that part
of DARPA, ARPA, which

00:53:27.780 --> 00:53:32.560
presided over the creation
of the ARPANET, IPTO.

00:53:32.560 --> 00:53:36.330
Licklider later
from 1962 to 64 was

00:53:36.330 --> 00:53:40.800
the head of IPTO, information
processing techniques

00:53:40.800 --> 00:53:41.880
office of DARPA.

00:53:41.880 --> 00:53:42.680
OK.

00:53:42.680 --> 00:53:44.580
But what I want to
stress, this man

00:53:44.580 --> 00:53:51.340
with a background in psychology
had a remarkably rich career

00:53:51.340 --> 00:53:57.600
that gave him to experience
from unusual capabilities.

00:53:57.600 --> 00:54:01.960
And maybe some of you have
had similar experiences.

00:54:01.960 --> 00:54:06.940
During World War II, and many of
the people who came to the fore

00:54:06.940 --> 00:54:08.920
in the period about
which I'm speaking,

00:54:08.920 --> 00:54:13.360
the '60s and the '50s, had
an interesting World War II

00:54:13.360 --> 00:54:16.470
experiences in the realm
of science and technology.

00:54:16.470 --> 00:54:18.850
World War II for
scientists and engineers

00:54:18.850 --> 00:54:21.640
was a remarkable
learning experience.

00:54:21.640 --> 00:54:23.380
Well, Licklider,
during World War II,

00:54:23.380 --> 00:54:26.350
had been a part of a
psycho-acoustics laboratory

00:54:26.350 --> 00:54:28.240
at Harvard University.

00:54:28.240 --> 00:54:31.140
Then after the war,
he moves to MIT

00:54:31.140 --> 00:54:34.440
to head an acoustics laboratory.

00:54:34.440 --> 00:54:37.350
How does a man move from
acoustics to computers?

00:54:37.350 --> 00:54:38.820
Well see.

00:54:38.820 --> 00:54:44.790
Now while at MIT, he became
involved in those organizations

00:54:44.790 --> 00:54:48.390
that were providing rich
experiences for those

00:54:48.390 --> 00:54:52.470
who would take the risk of
becoming part of these new

00:54:52.470 --> 00:54:54.540
and risk taking organizations.

00:54:54.540 --> 00:54:58.410
For example, he became
a research associate

00:54:58.410 --> 00:55:02.460
in the MIT research
laboratory of electronics.

00:55:02.460 --> 00:55:10.920
Now that laboratory was
famous in the 1950s and '60s

00:55:10.920 --> 00:55:13.560
for an interdisciplinary
approach.

00:55:13.560 --> 00:55:17.160
Interdisciplinary with
something still fairly new

00:55:17.160 --> 00:55:19.230
in the engineering world.

00:55:19.230 --> 00:55:22.890
So he becomes a part of
MIT research laboratory

00:55:22.890 --> 00:55:24.240
of electronics.

00:55:24.240 --> 00:55:28.360
He also takes part
in Project Charles.

00:55:28.360 --> 00:55:32.910
Project Charles was a
summer study project.

00:55:32.910 --> 00:55:36.640
We don't have these
projects anymore.

00:55:36.640 --> 00:55:42.640
Summer studies were very popular
at MIT in the '50s and '60s.

00:55:42.640 --> 00:55:44.920
What was a summer project?

00:55:44.920 --> 00:55:49.940
A summer project was funded
by the military, generously

00:55:49.940 --> 00:55:51.530
funded.

00:55:51.530 --> 00:55:55.520
The project involved inviting
a number of academics

00:55:55.520 --> 00:56:00.150
and industrial people and
military people, maybe 30,

00:56:00.150 --> 00:56:04.000
maybe 40, that
neighborhood number,

00:56:04.000 --> 00:56:07.220
to just take off from
their responsibilities

00:56:07.220 --> 00:56:08.450
for an entire summer.

00:56:08.450 --> 00:56:11.270
Later, it became
sometimes six months.

00:56:11.270 --> 00:56:16.730
And focus at a bucolic site,
geographical site, somewhere up

00:56:16.730 --> 00:56:19.040
in the mountains,
down to the seashore,

00:56:19.040 --> 00:56:22.190
these 40 or 60 people free
from their responsibilities

00:56:22.190 --> 00:56:25.520
would concentrate upon one
particular problem of interest

00:56:25.520 --> 00:56:30.630
to the military for
six months or a summer.

00:56:30.630 --> 00:56:33.610
And one of the major projects
was called Project Charles,

00:56:33.610 --> 00:56:36.810
which dedicated the
people who were members

00:56:36.810 --> 00:56:39.210
of Project Charles
dedicated themselves

00:56:39.210 --> 00:56:46.570
to setting up the air
defense system called SAGE.

00:56:46.570 --> 00:56:51.220
Well, Licklider took part in
one of these summer projects.

00:56:51.220 --> 00:56:55.450
And I'm told by people whom
I interviewed at the summer

00:56:55.450 --> 00:56:59.860
projects, that when you
entered the door wherever

00:56:59.860 --> 00:57:02.950
the meeting was being held,
that you were told to forget

00:57:02.950 --> 00:57:07.220
your discipline, to forget
that you were an expert,

00:57:07.220 --> 00:57:09.440
and that just focus
on the problem

00:57:09.440 --> 00:57:13.580
and go wherever the problem
took you into whatever field

00:57:13.580 --> 00:57:15.650
of engineering or
whatever field of science

00:57:15.650 --> 00:57:19.130
because there are no experts
in this area we're exploring.

00:57:19.130 --> 00:57:21.410
And incidentally, that
was the same technique

00:57:21.410 --> 00:57:25.800
used at the Rand Corporation
about the same time.

00:57:25.800 --> 00:57:27.750
Forget your expertise.

00:57:27.750 --> 00:57:31.470
Let the problem decide where
you would do your thinking.

00:57:31.470 --> 00:57:34.500
So Licklider was a part
of Project Charles.

00:57:34.500 --> 00:57:36.840
Also, he heard about
Norbert Wiener.

00:57:36.840 --> 00:57:39.240
I think all of you have
heard about Norbert Wiener.

00:57:39.240 --> 00:57:43.530
And so he became a part of the
group that would mix and meet

00:57:43.530 --> 00:57:44.910
with Norbert Wiener.

00:57:44.910 --> 00:57:49.780
So this man had a rich,
rich MIT experience.

00:57:49.780 --> 00:57:52.925
And after MIT and in electrical
engineering department

00:57:52.925 --> 00:57:56.610
and acoustics lab, he went
to Lincoln Laboratory.

00:57:56.610 --> 00:58:00.780
That was another great learning
experience in the '50s.

00:58:00.780 --> 00:58:03.030
Lincoln Laboratory
was established

00:58:03.030 --> 00:58:06.120
to do the research and
development for the SAGE air

00:58:06.120 --> 00:58:07.620
defense project.

00:58:07.620 --> 00:58:11.100
But it was funded
by the Air Force.

00:58:11.100 --> 00:58:16.804
It had some of the most
advanced computers in the world.

00:58:16.804 --> 00:58:19.345
And so if one had an opportunity
to go to Lincoln Laboratory,

00:58:19.345 --> 00:58:22.930
as you know which is
still administered by MIT,

00:58:22.930 --> 00:58:28.870
then one had hands on with the
most advanced technological

00:58:28.870 --> 00:58:31.540
and specialty
computer equipment.

00:58:31.540 --> 00:58:36.520
Then from there, he went
to Bolt Beranek and Newman,

00:58:36.520 --> 00:58:41.830
a high technology
firm founded in 1948.

00:58:41.830 --> 00:58:44.320
I think you're familiar
with this firm today.

00:58:44.320 --> 00:58:47.830
It's a major player in the
acoustics and the computing

00:58:47.830 --> 00:58:48.820
field.

00:58:48.820 --> 00:58:51.850
So he moves on to Bolt
Beranek and Newman.

00:58:51.850 --> 00:58:59.000
Rich experiences which prepared
him for his work at IPTO.

00:58:59.000 --> 00:59:02.280
Now Licklider was visionary.

00:59:02.280 --> 00:59:04.470
And what was his vision?

00:59:04.470 --> 00:59:10.290
He had a vision
in the mid 1950s,

00:59:10.290 --> 00:59:15.720
he had a vision of time
sharing on a computer.

00:59:15.720 --> 00:59:20.220
And he also had the vision
of interconnecting computers.

00:59:20.220 --> 00:59:26.680
And he often gathered together
people in the Cambridge area,

00:59:26.680 --> 00:59:30.200
maybe over a few drinks,
to talk about the future,

00:59:30.200 --> 00:59:32.980
the possibilities
of time sharing,

00:59:32.980 --> 00:59:36.070
the possibilities of
computer interconnection.

00:59:36.070 --> 00:59:39.670
He spoke a
man-computer symbiosis.

00:59:39.670 --> 00:59:43.450
Now Licklider, in this
earlier period, the 1950s,

00:59:43.450 --> 00:59:46.030
was not moving towards
the development

00:59:46.030 --> 00:59:50.530
of the computer as a
calculating arithmetic device.

00:59:50.530 --> 00:59:52.510
He was one of the pioneers.

00:59:52.510 --> 00:59:56.470
He saw that a computer could be
used for control and decision

00:59:56.470 --> 00:59:58.790
making and other purposes.

00:59:58.790 --> 00:59:59.890
So the man has vision.

00:59:59.890 --> 01:00:01.540
He has a number of followers.

01:00:01.540 --> 01:00:11.170
And now I would like to show
you the Licklider network.

01:00:11.170 --> 01:00:16.220
I hope you have it before
you on one of the charts.

01:00:16.220 --> 01:00:20.220
To me, this is a
remarkable network.

01:00:20.220 --> 01:00:24.170
Please note the movements
of these people.

01:00:24.170 --> 01:00:26.610
These are people
with, most of whom,

01:00:26.610 --> 01:00:30.900
Licklider is
intimately associated.

01:00:30.900 --> 01:00:34.530
And the majority of them
have similar experiences

01:00:34.530 --> 01:00:40.440
to which he had, MIT, electrical
engineering, Lincoln Lab.

01:00:40.440 --> 01:00:45.320
Some of them at Bolt
Beranek and Newman.

01:00:45.320 --> 01:00:52.110
This was the context in which
computer pioneers who made

01:00:52.110 --> 01:00:55.880
the ARPANET learned the trade.

01:00:55.880 --> 01:01:01.100
And note the movements of
a relatively few people

01:01:01.100 --> 01:01:04.580
and the organizations with
which they were associated.

01:01:07.700 --> 01:01:15.680
Now turning to the
choice of problem,

01:01:15.680 --> 01:01:19.660
choice to work on the ARPANET.

01:01:19.660 --> 01:01:25.890
Rather simple origin,
ARPA was funding

01:01:25.890 --> 01:01:31.030
a number of computer
research centers

01:01:31.030 --> 01:01:36.300
in the United States
in major universities,

01:01:36.300 --> 01:01:42.610
such as Carnegie Mellon,
MIT, UCLA, Stanford Research

01:01:42.610 --> 01:01:46.050
Laboratory, and so forth.

01:01:46.050 --> 01:01:49.830
ARPA was funding these
computer research centers.

01:01:49.830 --> 01:01:52.350
Actually, these were
computer research centers

01:01:52.350 --> 01:01:59.800
which Licklider had
his network located.

01:01:59.800 --> 01:02:04.870
And he funded his network
through these computer centers.

01:02:04.870 --> 01:02:09.360
Now the cost of the
computers is very high.

01:02:09.360 --> 01:02:14.430
And it was thought that if
one could bring these centers

01:02:14.430 --> 01:02:20.020
to share their computers, to
interconnecting the computers,

01:02:20.020 --> 01:02:23.940
ARPA costs would be reduced.

01:02:23.940 --> 01:02:29.370
And according to Robert Taylor,
who was one of the IPTO heads,

01:02:29.370 --> 01:02:34.020
the idea for this cost
saving interconnection

01:02:34.020 --> 01:02:37.100
came to him when he was sitting
in his office in Washington.

01:02:37.100 --> 01:02:38.880
Remember he's an IPTO head.

01:02:38.880 --> 01:02:43.410
And he had terminals
from three time sharing

01:02:43.410 --> 01:02:45.490
computers in his office.

01:02:45.490 --> 01:02:48.930
I think one of the time
sharing computers was at MIT.

01:02:48.930 --> 01:02:52.249
Another may have been somewhere
in the Washington area.

01:02:52.249 --> 01:02:52.790
I'm not sure.

01:02:52.790 --> 01:02:56.080
We had three terminals from
three time sharing computers.

01:02:56.080 --> 01:02:58.121
And they were not
interconnected.

01:02:58.121 --> 01:02:59.620
And he said, well,
the obvious thing

01:02:59.620 --> 01:03:03.010
is to interconnect the other
time sharing computers.

01:03:03.010 --> 01:03:06.700
And the need, of course,
is to reduce cost

01:03:06.700 --> 01:03:15.770
by sharing computer capacity
and sharing software.

01:03:15.770 --> 01:03:22.680
Now a word about this is the
inauguration of the project.

01:03:22.680 --> 01:03:28.090
The conceptual
design of the project

01:03:28.090 --> 01:03:33.830
was worked out by a
distributed committee.

01:03:33.830 --> 01:03:38.870
In the case of Edison, who
had the people who came up

01:03:38.870 --> 01:03:41.540
with the conceptual design,
that thought process,

01:03:41.540 --> 01:03:45.260
that reasoning I went through,
they were located in one place.

01:03:45.260 --> 01:03:48.420
They were in a hierarchical
structure headed by Edison.

01:03:48.420 --> 01:03:52.370
But it is remarkable that
the conceptual design

01:03:52.370 --> 01:03:57.770
for the ARPANET came about
through informal meetings

01:03:57.770 --> 01:04:00.320
of the heads of these
various computer

01:04:00.320 --> 01:04:04.430
centers, informal meetings
called by Licklider.

01:04:04.430 --> 01:04:08.480
And Licklider, in one of the
interviews that I've used,

01:04:08.480 --> 01:04:12.080
said, all of the heads
of the research centers

01:04:12.080 --> 01:04:15.530
would gather at some place
for a committee meeting,

01:04:15.530 --> 01:04:18.260
a committee of research heads.

01:04:18.260 --> 01:04:21.170
And we'd have some alcohol
in the late evening.

01:04:21.170 --> 01:04:24.890
And then I might bring up the
subject of computer networking.

01:04:24.890 --> 01:04:26.900
And we'd stay up
into the late hours.

01:04:26.900 --> 01:04:33.320
And then perhaps an idea for the
IMP, the information processor,

01:04:33.320 --> 01:04:35.660
Wesley Roberts would
come up with that.

01:04:35.660 --> 01:04:38.450
And then somebody might
mention packet switching,

01:04:38.450 --> 01:04:40.220
and we would talk about that.

01:04:40.220 --> 01:04:45.650
So what Licklider was
doing is with his network,

01:04:45.650 --> 01:04:49.920
he was drawing out of them
the ideas through discussion

01:04:49.920 --> 01:04:55.640
that would become the conceptual
design of the ARPANET.

01:04:55.640 --> 01:04:59.210
Now the next step
in that procedure

01:04:59.210 --> 01:05:02.390
was that IPTO, the office
that would preside over

01:05:02.390 --> 01:05:10.070
this development, IPTO sent
out a call, a request for bids

01:05:10.070 --> 01:05:11.840
to a number of companies.

01:05:11.840 --> 01:05:16.370
And the request for bids had
this basic conceptual design

01:05:16.370 --> 01:05:18.060
in the request.

01:05:18.060 --> 01:05:21.590
We want a computer network
with so many nodes.

01:05:21.590 --> 01:05:24.320
We want to use packet switching.

01:05:24.320 --> 01:05:30.260
We also are interested in
having interface computers that

01:05:30.260 --> 01:05:33.380
will allow their host
computers, although they

01:05:33.380 --> 01:05:35.530
have different design,
to interconnect.

01:05:35.530 --> 01:05:37.530
Even though they have
different characteristics,

01:05:37.530 --> 01:05:40.130
they will interconnect
to the telephone network

01:05:40.130 --> 01:05:44.390
through these IMPs, these
interface computers.

01:05:44.390 --> 01:05:49.010
These ideas were coming up
through these discussions

01:05:49.010 --> 01:05:52.390
that took place over
a number of months.

01:05:52.390 --> 01:05:59.620
Now the fundamental
contract, the basic contract

01:05:59.620 --> 01:06:02.740
for the design of
the IMPs was given

01:06:02.740 --> 01:06:07.980
to Bolt Beranek and Newman,
the company which you remember

01:06:07.980 --> 01:06:12.390
Licklider was a researcher
at and then later

01:06:12.390 --> 01:06:14.650
a vice president of.

01:06:14.650 --> 01:06:17.710
So this company had
the responsibility

01:06:17.710 --> 01:06:24.825
for developing the
interface computer, the IMP.

01:06:24.825 --> 01:06:29.180
Now I want to just say a
word as a move to conclusion

01:06:29.180 --> 01:06:35.220
about the style
of research at BBN

01:06:35.220 --> 01:06:38.490
because I think this
style of research at BBN

01:06:38.490 --> 01:06:44.190
has become a rather commonplace
style in the world of projects

01:06:44.190 --> 01:06:44.690
today.

01:06:44.690 --> 01:06:46.935
You can tell me whether
this is true or not.

01:06:46.935 --> 01:06:54.390
And let me read a quote from
Robert Kahn who was at BBN.

01:06:54.390 --> 01:06:57.450
He said, BBN-- this
is from an interview.

01:06:57.450 --> 01:07:03.384
BBN was a kind of hybrid version
of Harvard and MIT in the sense

01:07:03.384 --> 01:07:04.800
that most of the
people there were

01:07:04.800 --> 01:07:08.890
either faculty or former faculty
at either Harvard or MIT.

01:07:08.890 --> 01:07:11.880
The role that MIT
plays in this episode

01:07:11.880 --> 01:07:15.287
is a remarkably active one.

01:07:15.287 --> 01:07:17.620
If you've ever spent any time
in either of those places,

01:07:17.620 --> 01:07:21.490
you would know what a unique
kind of organization BBN was.

01:07:21.490 --> 01:07:24.700
A lot of the students at those
places spent time at BBN.

01:07:24.700 --> 01:07:26.320
It was kind of like
a super hyped up

01:07:26.320 --> 01:07:29.411
version of the union of
the two, Harvard and MIT,

01:07:29.411 --> 01:07:30.910
except that you
didn't have to worry

01:07:30.910 --> 01:07:32.500
about classes and teaching.

01:07:32.500 --> 01:07:34.330
You could just
focus on research.

01:07:34.330 --> 01:07:37.080
It was sort of the cognac
of the research business,

01:07:37.080 --> 01:07:38.500
very distilled.

01:07:38.500 --> 01:07:41.440
The culture of BBN at the time
was to do interesting things

01:07:41.440 --> 01:07:43.930
and to move on to the
next interesting things.

01:07:43.930 --> 01:07:47.740
And there was more incentive to
come up with interesting ideas

01:07:47.740 --> 01:07:50.710
and explore them than to try to
capitalize on them once they'd

01:07:50.710 --> 01:07:52.120
been developed.

01:07:52.120 --> 01:07:58.120
So BBN will develop the hardware
and some of the software

01:07:58.120 --> 01:07:59.390
for the ARPANET.

01:07:59.390 --> 01:08:03.760
And also one more quote
about the style and BBN.

01:08:03.760 --> 01:08:06.460
One of the team
managers, Hart, said,

01:08:06.460 --> 01:08:09.130
what we were doing in
developing this hardware

01:08:09.130 --> 01:08:11.180
was a labor of love.

01:08:11.180 --> 01:08:12.760
It was all fun.

01:08:12.760 --> 01:08:16.790
And I have enough collaborating
evidence to accept this.

01:08:16.790 --> 01:08:19.810
And we all on the team,
a team of 10 or 15

01:08:19.810 --> 01:08:21.729
at BBN, we thought holistically.

01:08:21.729 --> 01:08:25.270
We reached our
decisions by consensus.

01:08:25.270 --> 01:08:26.979
The team leader would
only come in when

01:08:26.979 --> 01:08:29.710
you couldn't reach a consensus.

01:08:29.710 --> 01:08:32.529
There was no
micromanagement here.

01:08:32.529 --> 01:08:35.275
We were suitably black boxed.

01:08:38.109 --> 01:08:40.840
Now another interview with
one of the persons at BBN

01:08:40.840 --> 01:08:44.050
said, when a breakthrough
occurred, we'd run in

01:08:44.050 --> 01:08:45.670
and say, look, I
got this running.

01:08:45.670 --> 01:08:47.979
Somebody come and type
this on the teletype.

01:08:47.979 --> 01:08:49.330
This is exciting.

01:08:49.330 --> 01:08:52.069
Something is cycling.

01:08:52.069 --> 01:08:56.560
Now another characteristic of
this research that was going on

01:08:56.560 --> 01:08:59.710
at ARPA was the meritocracy.

01:08:59.710 --> 01:09:01.819
It wasn't a
hierarchical activity.

01:09:01.819 --> 01:09:03.340
And one more quote.

01:09:03.340 --> 01:09:09.240
A group of graduate students
were set up as a committee

01:09:09.240 --> 01:09:11.550
at another site than BBN,
but this type of thing

01:09:11.550 --> 01:09:13.020
went on with BBN too.

01:09:13.020 --> 01:09:15.029
A group of graduate
students was set up

01:09:15.029 --> 01:09:17.740
to develop the protocols
for the internet.

01:09:17.740 --> 01:09:20.250
And here's a quote from one
of the graduate students.

01:09:20.250 --> 01:09:21.960
We were just rank
amateurs and we

01:09:21.960 --> 01:09:24.330
were expecting that some
authority would finally

01:09:24.330 --> 01:09:27.390
come along and say, here's
how we're going to do it.

01:09:27.390 --> 01:09:29.687
A professor would
come along and say,

01:09:29.687 --> 01:09:31.020
here's how we're going to do it.

01:09:31.020 --> 01:09:32.880
And nobody ever came along.

01:09:32.880 --> 01:09:35.490
So we were sort of
tentatively feeling our way

01:09:35.490 --> 01:09:36.930
into how we could
go about getting

01:09:36.930 --> 01:09:39.590
this software up and running.

01:09:39.590 --> 01:09:41.810
And there are many,
many, many examples

01:09:41.810 --> 01:09:45.390
of this sort of
flat organization.

01:09:45.390 --> 01:09:49.370
Now in conclusion,
I want to show you--

01:09:49.370 --> 01:09:50.910
don't show the ARPANET.

01:09:50.910 --> 01:09:52.819
Well, yes, quickly
the ARPANET diagrams.

01:09:55.760 --> 01:09:56.950
These are historic.

01:09:56.950 --> 01:10:00.970
This is the first
diagram I encountered.

01:10:00.970 --> 01:10:02.920
It was done in longhand.

01:10:02.920 --> 01:10:05.290
And it showed one IMP.

01:10:05.290 --> 01:10:06.790
That's the interface
computer that

01:10:06.790 --> 01:10:09.530
connects the host with
the telephone network.

01:10:09.530 --> 01:10:11.260
And one host computer.

01:10:11.260 --> 01:10:13.150
Let's see the next one.

01:10:13.150 --> 01:10:15.790
This is by August 1972.

01:10:15.790 --> 01:10:20.300
The other was in a few years
earlier, three years earlier.

01:10:20.300 --> 01:10:22.780
And this is a network
as it was in 1972.

01:10:22.780 --> 01:10:27.650
I like to show this because the
growth of the computer network

01:10:27.650 --> 01:10:30.430
like the growth of
a railroad network.

01:10:30.430 --> 01:10:32.080
Next one please.

01:10:32.080 --> 01:10:35.380
And in conclusion,
something I hope

01:10:35.380 --> 01:10:39.210
we can talk about
it at another time.

01:10:39.210 --> 01:10:42.550
I wasn't able to give you
all of the information I'd

01:10:42.550 --> 01:10:44.900
like to have given you
about the style of BBN,

01:10:44.900 --> 01:10:46.850
the ARPA style of management.

01:10:46.850 --> 01:10:50.560
I hope I indicated and
suggested it to you the style.

01:10:50.560 --> 01:10:53.260
But out of the study
I've done, I've

01:10:53.260 --> 01:10:56.230
come up with a contrast
between the kind of production

01:10:56.230 --> 01:10:59.530
management that took
place, say, at Ford Motor

01:10:59.530 --> 01:11:04.300
Company in the 1920s and the
kind of project management

01:11:04.300 --> 01:11:07.480
that I count on
these projects today.

01:11:07.480 --> 01:11:11.920
And let's run very quickly
through the contrast.

01:11:11.920 --> 01:11:15.260
Before World War
II, hierarchical.

01:11:15.260 --> 01:11:17.500
Now today, flat.

01:11:17.500 --> 01:11:20.140
Specialization
was well regarded.

01:11:20.140 --> 01:11:22.360
Now it's interdisciplinary.

01:11:22.360 --> 01:11:24.640
Integration over coordination.

01:11:24.640 --> 01:11:27.770
Rational order
was the objective.

01:11:27.770 --> 01:11:29.900
Now messy complexity
is accepted.

01:11:29.900 --> 01:11:33.170
Heterogeneity is accepted.

01:11:33.170 --> 01:11:34.880
We could go on down the list.

01:11:34.880 --> 01:11:36.740
Experts were highly regarded.

01:11:36.740 --> 01:11:39.560
Now it's more of a meritocracy
as the anecdote I gave you

01:11:39.560 --> 01:11:41.570
about the graduate students.

01:11:41.570 --> 01:11:43.070
Tightly coupled to a network.

01:11:43.070 --> 01:11:44.810
Let's have the next
one very quickly.

01:11:44.810 --> 01:11:49.457
And then program control
against feedback control.

01:11:49.457 --> 01:11:51.040
Systems engineering
against taylorism.

01:11:51.040 --> 01:11:58.130
And it seems to me as
we move into the world

01:11:58.130 --> 01:12:01.010
where projects play
an increasing role,

01:12:01.010 --> 01:12:03.800
these are some of
the characteristics

01:12:03.800 --> 01:12:05.990
of project management.

01:12:05.990 --> 01:12:08.444
Thank you, Tom.

01:12:08.444 --> 01:12:14.827
[APPLAUSE]

01:12:18.264 --> 01:12:21.910
MAGNANTI: We have a few minutes
for questions or commentary

01:12:21.910 --> 01:12:26.680
either from people here at
MIT or people at a distance.

01:12:26.680 --> 01:12:29.650
Anyone like to add or ask
any questions or comment?

01:12:32.999 --> 01:12:34.540
AUDIENCE: Professor,
I'd like to know

01:12:34.540 --> 01:12:37.870
if there's plans to post
the slides that were shown?

01:12:37.870 --> 01:12:41.457
Some were a bit difficult
to read from a distance.

01:12:41.457 --> 01:12:42.040
MAGNANTI: Yes.

01:12:42.040 --> 01:12:45.090
Yes, we do plan to
post the slides.

01:12:45.090 --> 01:12:47.020
So they will be available
for everyone, yes.

01:12:49.609 --> 01:12:51.150
AUDIENCE: Secondly,
I have a question

01:12:51.150 --> 01:12:55.290
about the Thomas Edison slide
showing the organization that

01:12:55.290 --> 01:12:59.490
was early in the presentation.

01:12:59.490 --> 01:13:02.130
You showed that as being
separate companies,

01:13:02.130 --> 01:13:05.490
or was that the forerunner
to Ford's concept

01:13:05.490 --> 01:13:09.450
that was very highly
vertically integrated?

01:13:09.450 --> 01:13:11.220
It wasn't unclear to me?

01:13:11.220 --> 01:13:14.980
HUGHES: You're speaking
of the Edison companies?

01:13:14.980 --> 01:13:18.490
I think you're speaking of the
Edison companies, the ones that

01:13:18.490 --> 01:13:21.370
were established, like the
Edison Manufacturing Company

01:13:21.370 --> 01:13:24.460
and the utilities and so forth.

01:13:24.460 --> 01:13:29.450
These companies it
wasn't a vertical.

01:13:29.450 --> 01:13:31.720
No, it wasn't vertical.

01:13:31.720 --> 01:13:33.880
It was horizontal.

01:13:33.880 --> 01:13:35.950
The companies interacted.

01:13:35.950 --> 01:13:41.730
But there wasn't a
central management in 1882

01:13:41.730 --> 01:13:44.470
when these companies were
established to manufacture

01:13:44.470 --> 01:13:47.140
the generators, and the
companies established

01:13:47.140 --> 01:13:50.890
to manufacture the light
bulbs and the underground

01:13:50.890 --> 01:13:52.510
distribution and so forth.

01:13:52.510 --> 01:13:53.810
No, it was a horizontal.

01:13:53.810 --> 01:13:59.230
It was a horizontal arrangement
with the parent company,

01:13:59.230 --> 01:14:01.660
the Edison Electric Light
Company, the patent holding

01:14:01.660 --> 01:14:06.400
company, the investment
company having

01:14:06.400 --> 01:14:08.710
stock interest in
these companies

01:14:08.710 --> 01:14:10.660
and some control
through stock interests.

01:14:10.660 --> 01:14:14.770
But it was a loosely coupled
organizational system.

01:14:18.865 --> 01:14:19.730
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

01:14:22.670 --> 01:14:24.630
MAGNANTI: Please come
down to the mic, Joann.

01:14:33.045 --> 01:14:34.420
AUDIENCE: One of
the terms that I

01:14:34.420 --> 01:14:37.830
was looking for in this
comparison that I didn't see

01:14:37.830 --> 01:14:38.980
was emergent.

01:14:43.180 --> 01:14:45.820
The project management
that you described in ARPA

01:14:45.820 --> 01:14:49.150
seemed to me a much more,
at least in this project,

01:14:49.150 --> 01:14:51.370
a much more emergent
kind of thing.

01:14:51.370 --> 01:14:53.830
Because as we all
know, the ARPANET

01:14:53.830 --> 01:14:56.020
wasn't intended to
be a communication

01:14:56.020 --> 01:14:59.800
network originally, and of
course that emerged and so on.

01:14:59.800 --> 01:15:01.690
The graduate
students who sort of

01:15:01.690 --> 01:15:04.630
developed their own
standards and so on.

01:15:04.630 --> 01:15:07.690
Could we add emergent to
this project management?

01:15:07.690 --> 01:15:11.590
HUGHES: We certainly
could if you would define.

01:15:11.590 --> 01:15:14.260
AUDIENCE: Emerging over
time out of the interaction

01:15:14.260 --> 01:15:17.260
of the various socio technical
elements, but not managed,

01:15:17.260 --> 01:15:19.540
not planned in advance.

01:15:19.540 --> 01:15:20.050
Yeah.

01:15:20.050 --> 01:15:20.850
HUGHES: Yes.

01:15:20.850 --> 01:15:24.790
Yes, it certainly was,
Joann, it certainly

01:15:24.790 --> 01:15:28.211
was a emergent project.

01:15:28.211 --> 01:15:29.710
Because at the
beginning, as I said,

01:15:29.710 --> 01:15:33.700
the objective was to lower
cost, the primary objective.

01:15:33.700 --> 01:15:36.460
I haven't talked about
the military background.

01:15:36.460 --> 01:15:39.400
The primary immediate
objective was

01:15:39.400 --> 01:15:43.240
to lower costs through the
sharing of computer resources

01:15:43.240 --> 01:15:45.190
and software resources.

01:15:45.190 --> 01:15:48.970
But as you point out,
and within a few years

01:15:48.970 --> 01:15:54.430
after that demonstration
of the ARPANET in 1972,

01:15:54.430 --> 01:16:02.020
within a few years, email became
the major traffic generator

01:16:02.020 --> 01:16:03.310
on the ARPANET.

01:16:03.310 --> 01:16:06.730
And there was no
intention at the beginning

01:16:06.730 --> 01:16:11.170
of it using it primarily
as a mode of communication.

01:16:11.170 --> 01:16:12.670
And then also, I
think as you know,

01:16:12.670 --> 01:16:14.294
I didn't go into this
because I assumed

01:16:14.294 --> 01:16:22.150
you knew it, the ARPANET
after a while was turned over.

01:16:22.150 --> 01:16:25.345
The military took over
the military aspects

01:16:25.345 --> 01:16:25.970
of the ARPANET.

01:16:25.970 --> 01:16:28.210
And the other parts
of it were turned over

01:16:28.210 --> 01:16:30.100
to civilian agencies.

01:16:30.100 --> 01:16:36.730
NSF, for example, became a major
funder of computer networks.

01:16:36.730 --> 01:16:40.370
And so the ARPANET was
the one at the core.

01:16:40.370 --> 01:16:42.430
But then a number
of other networks

01:16:42.430 --> 01:16:45.670
funded by other agencies
were joined through protocol

01:16:45.670 --> 01:16:48.840
development to the ARPANET.

01:16:48.840 --> 01:16:51.160
So that wasn't expected either.

01:16:51.160 --> 01:16:53.410
It's good to stress this.

01:16:53.410 --> 01:16:56.970
And Edison didn't foresee many
of the future developments

01:16:56.970 --> 01:16:58.190
in electric light and power.

01:16:58.190 --> 01:16:59.920
He didn't see
alternating current.

01:16:59.920 --> 01:17:04.390
He did not see regional electric
light and power systems.

01:17:04.390 --> 01:17:06.494
I'm glad you made
the point, Joann.

01:17:11.901 --> 01:17:13.400
MAGNANTI: Other
questions, comments?

01:17:24.052 --> 01:17:25.760
AUDIENCE: Tom, I'm
trying to get a better

01:17:25.760 --> 01:17:28.730
grasp on the difference between
an open and a closed system.

01:17:28.730 --> 01:17:31.340
Would you characterize
the ARPANET project

01:17:31.340 --> 01:17:34.280
as an open system
or a closed one

01:17:34.280 --> 01:17:37.340
originally as it started out?

01:17:37.340 --> 01:17:39.740
HUGHES: Yes.

01:17:39.740 --> 01:17:41.790
I want to be sure this is on.

01:17:41.790 --> 01:17:44.730
The ARPANET I call
an open system

01:17:44.730 --> 01:17:48.840
because IPTO established
a number of these research

01:17:48.840 --> 01:17:50.610
centers.

01:17:50.610 --> 01:17:55.350
And the research centers were
the organizational framework

01:17:55.350 --> 01:18:00.750
within which the technical
network was housed.

01:18:00.750 --> 01:18:07.410
So the ARPANET involved not
only the technical aspects,

01:18:07.410 --> 01:18:11.880
the computers, the
telephone system, et cetera,

01:18:11.880 --> 01:18:14.550
but it also involved
the organizations,

01:18:14.550 --> 01:18:18.510
the research centers at
the various universities.

01:18:18.510 --> 01:18:21.310
And you couldn't have
one without the other.

01:18:21.310 --> 01:18:27.180
So people at IPTO had to
nurture the organizations

01:18:27.180 --> 01:18:28.980
at the same time
they were nurturing

01:18:28.980 --> 01:18:30.950
the technical content.

01:18:30.950 --> 01:18:33.780
And it was also an open
system because the designers

01:18:33.780 --> 01:18:40.650
of the system had to clear
the funding through Congress.

01:18:40.650 --> 01:18:46.290
Also, the Air Force, the
military generally was funding

01:18:46.290 --> 01:18:51.690
and funding had to be
arranged, et cetera.

01:18:51.690 --> 01:18:56.010
So it was a complex system that
involved technical components,

01:18:56.010 --> 01:18:58.770
funding components,
political problems,

01:18:58.770 --> 01:19:01.340
organizational components.

01:19:01.340 --> 01:19:03.920
And that's the point
I am trying to stress

01:19:03.920 --> 01:19:06.770
is most of the
technical systems you

01:19:06.770 --> 01:19:10.040
deal with are embedded in
a larger structure that

01:19:10.040 --> 01:19:12.140
can be called an open system.

01:19:12.140 --> 01:19:16.020
And as Joann points out,
these open systems often

01:19:16.020 --> 01:19:18.080
are emergent systems
and have many

01:19:18.080 --> 01:19:20.750
of these complicated
characteristics,

01:19:20.750 --> 01:19:23.536
extremely difficult to
manage once they're in place.

01:19:23.536 --> 01:19:24.202
AUDIENCE: Great.

01:19:24.202 --> 01:19:25.174
Thanks, Tom.

01:19:31.500 --> 01:19:36.690
HUGHES: And because of this
audience, I'll ask you.

01:19:36.690 --> 01:19:39.960
Do you find that
projects are increasingly

01:19:39.960 --> 01:19:45.030
common in the organizations
within which you work?

01:19:45.030 --> 01:19:51.640
I gave you the analogy
of the theater where

01:19:51.640 --> 01:19:56.560
a group is brought together
for a specific length of time

01:19:56.560 --> 01:19:59.650
to solve one particular problem.

01:19:59.650 --> 01:20:02.260
And the people on the group
may come from various parts

01:20:02.260 --> 01:20:04.150
of the organization or company.

01:20:04.150 --> 01:20:06.920
Then after the
project is fulfilled,

01:20:06.920 --> 01:20:11.260
the goals are met, then the
project disbands and people go

01:20:11.260 --> 01:20:16.750
back to their respective
areas of commitment

01:20:16.750 --> 01:20:19.566
or wherever their home base is.

01:20:39.360 --> 01:20:42.660
AUDIENCE: I have a question, but
my thoughts are spinning such,

01:20:42.660 --> 01:20:47.290
I hope I can
clarify it in a way.

01:20:47.290 --> 01:20:49.890
I'm affiliated with the
Air Force right now.

01:20:49.890 --> 01:20:56.820
And my questions go back to the
idea of the conceptual design

01:20:56.820 --> 01:20:59.490
and how you talked
about the network that

01:20:59.490 --> 01:21:06.240
was among these people and the
loose style that was there.

01:21:06.240 --> 01:21:09.600
In the research I've
been doing, I've

01:21:09.600 --> 01:21:12.990
been seeing the Air Force
has tried to capture

01:21:12.990 --> 01:21:15.720
these networks of people.

01:21:15.720 --> 01:21:18.270
But they're not being
very successful.

01:21:18.270 --> 01:21:21.300
Instead, what's happening
is those projects

01:21:21.300 --> 01:21:27.270
that are getting funded
are from a different kind

01:21:27.270 --> 01:21:32.595
of a person that has another
network, an informal network.

01:21:32.595 --> 01:21:34.470
I guess what I'm trying
to say, the Air Force

01:21:34.470 --> 01:21:39.900
has tried to formalize and
structure and establish

01:21:39.900 --> 01:21:43.350
networks to emulate
some of these attributes

01:21:43.350 --> 01:21:44.880
that you've talked about.

01:21:44.880 --> 01:21:46.050
But they're failing.

01:21:46.050 --> 01:21:49.492
And it's this
informal network is

01:21:49.492 --> 01:21:50.700
the one that's still working.

01:21:50.700 --> 01:21:56.280
And I'd be interested in your
comments on that and maybe

01:21:56.280 --> 01:21:59.730
because it's something the
Air Force is struggling with.

01:21:59.730 --> 01:22:01.980
HUGHES: I'm glad you brought
that up because it allows

01:22:01.980 --> 01:22:06.420
me to go back to Licklider.

01:22:06.420 --> 01:22:09.480
I think I showed this
diagram, which you have,

01:22:09.480 --> 01:22:12.510
of the Licklider network.

01:22:12.510 --> 01:22:18.150
And Licklider cultivated
this network informally.

01:22:18.150 --> 01:22:21.660
But he also
structured it formally

01:22:21.660 --> 01:22:28.950
in that he would help the people
in his network become located

01:22:28.950 --> 01:22:31.770
in these research centers.

01:22:31.770 --> 01:22:37.040
And then he would also, when
he had this funding resource

01:22:37.040 --> 01:22:40.620
at IPTO, which is
part of ARPA, he

01:22:40.620 --> 01:22:44.330
would fund people
in his network who

01:22:44.330 --> 01:22:48.170
were located in these
various research centers.

01:22:48.170 --> 01:22:51.950
But again, on the informal
level, again, as I said,

01:22:51.950 --> 01:22:53.770
his people would
often gather together

01:22:53.770 --> 01:22:55.640
at meetings he
would call and talk

01:22:55.640 --> 01:22:58.470
into the late
hours about concept

01:22:58.470 --> 01:23:02.810
for time sharing or concept
computer networking.

01:23:02.810 --> 01:23:07.530
But so he was playing the
informal and the formal.

01:23:07.530 --> 01:23:10.070
Formal being, again,
placing the people

01:23:10.070 --> 01:23:13.650
in an organizational structure,
these research centers,

01:23:13.650 --> 01:23:15.330
for example.

01:23:15.330 --> 01:23:22.490
So I think the most direct
answer to your very interesting

01:23:22.490 --> 01:23:27.146
question is in government
today, project managers,

01:23:27.146 --> 01:23:28.520
and that's what
Licklider was, he

01:23:28.520 --> 01:23:33.170
was a project manager for
ARPA, are extremely important.

01:23:33.170 --> 01:23:36.590
And there are project managers
in the Air Force, as you know.

01:23:36.590 --> 01:23:38.870
But I've been told by
some of the people whom

01:23:38.870 --> 01:23:43.460
I've interviewed that the
project managers today are not

01:23:43.460 --> 01:23:47.810
given the freedom and the leeway
to organize informal networks,

01:23:47.810 --> 01:23:53.690
to draw out conceptual designs
as freely and imaginatively

01:23:53.690 --> 01:23:56.150
as they once were able to do.

01:23:56.150 --> 01:23:59.150
So I think the answer
to your question

01:23:59.150 --> 01:24:04.340
is put your attention upon
the functioning of the project

01:24:04.340 --> 01:24:06.860
managers, people like
Licklider in Air Force,

01:24:06.860 --> 01:24:08.690
and what kind of
success are they having?

01:24:08.690 --> 01:24:11.240
What kind of reinforcement
are they getting?

01:24:11.240 --> 01:24:15.500
What sort of freedom
of action do they have?

01:24:15.500 --> 01:24:19.430
And again, I repeat, I've been
told that the project managers

01:24:19.430 --> 01:24:26.300
do not have the freedom
to maneuver and to develop

01:24:26.300 --> 01:24:29.300
and feel imaginative
concepts that they once

01:24:29.300 --> 01:24:32.300
had because of a number of
bureaucratic constraints

01:24:32.300 --> 01:24:34.330
and regulations.

01:24:34.330 --> 01:24:37.370
And I think I know what you
mean by an informal network.

01:24:37.370 --> 01:24:40.970
That is a special
interest group that

01:24:40.970 --> 01:24:47.240
is responding to the needs of,
say, a particular small group.

01:24:47.240 --> 01:24:51.530
And you don't have this
drawing upon a community

01:24:51.530 --> 01:24:55.516
that Licklider drew upon.

01:24:55.516 --> 01:24:56.016
Yes.

01:25:04.000 --> 01:25:05.830
MAGNANTI: Let me, on
behalf of all of us,

01:25:05.830 --> 01:25:10.570
thank Professor Hughes for
this fascinating account of two

01:25:10.570 --> 01:25:12.040
remarkably different projects.

01:25:12.040 --> 01:25:13.330
I think as you explained them.

01:25:13.330 --> 01:25:16.019
Whether one is the old fashioned
way of doing things, the Edison

01:25:16.019 --> 01:25:17.810
way, and the ARPA is
the new fashioned way,

01:25:17.810 --> 01:25:20.560
I think is one for all
of us to think about.

01:25:20.560 --> 01:25:23.860
But the contrast is
rather stark, I would say,

01:25:23.860 --> 01:25:26.700
in terms of the differences
in the organizational style

01:25:26.700 --> 01:25:28.840
and the approach for
these two projects.

01:25:28.840 --> 01:25:31.600
Both eminently successful
in terms of their projects.

01:25:31.600 --> 01:25:37.840
I would recommend, again, to
everyone to Professor Hughes'

01:25:37.840 --> 01:25:41.860
book, particularly those of
you who are interested in MIT,

01:25:41.860 --> 01:25:43.690
because there's a lot
of MIT in this book.

01:25:43.690 --> 01:25:47.020
I'm really impressed as you
look through it the impact

01:25:47.020 --> 01:25:48.672
I think that MIT has had.

01:25:48.672 --> 01:25:50.380
And all of us should
feel good about this

01:25:50.380 --> 01:25:52.660
in terms of the way of
thinking about complex systems.

01:25:52.660 --> 01:25:55.480
This has been an organization
that's been deeply immersed

01:25:55.480 --> 01:25:57.370
in this for many, many years.

01:25:57.370 --> 01:26:01.210
As I said at the outset of
this presentation today,

01:26:01.210 --> 01:26:03.340
Professor Hughes is on
campus for the semester

01:26:03.340 --> 01:26:05.550
as a guest of MIT.

01:26:05.550 --> 01:26:08.080
He will be around in
the MIT community.

01:26:08.080 --> 01:26:09.790
I think delighted
to meet with anyone

01:26:09.790 --> 01:26:11.980
who might like to talk to
him further about these.

01:26:11.980 --> 01:26:14.879
He will be giving another
public lecture on October 30

01:26:14.879 --> 01:26:17.170
to pursue some of the topics
that he talked about today

01:26:17.170 --> 01:26:19.420
and I think talk about some
other of these large scale

01:26:19.420 --> 01:26:20.021
projects.

01:26:20.021 --> 01:26:22.270
And he'll be meeting with
the SDM students in private.

01:26:22.270 --> 01:26:24.276
So please join me
again in thanking

01:26:24.276 --> 01:26:26.650
Professor Hughes for this very
illuminating presentation.

01:26:26.650 --> 01:26:30.600
[APPLAUSE]

